md`# SoundScript specification

This page defines SoundScript. It tells you what a cell is, what the checker accepts and rejects, and what the notebook does with cells when they run and when you edit them. SoundScript is a checked subset of TypeScript for notebook cells. A cell is a function, and its parameters are the cells that it reads. Start with the [tutorial](${linkTo("@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-tutorial")}). If the code has a behaviour that this page does not state, that behaviour is not promised. The last section lists the known cases.`
// %%
md`Each statement has a number, such as **S3.2**. The number stays the same when statements are added. A section has four parts, in this order:

1. its statements;
2. cells in SoundScript that the statements describe;
3. a table of sources that the checker must reject, with the rule that each must give;
4. tests.

A test is a cell that has the name of the statement that it checks: \`test_spec_S3_2\` checks S3.2. For each section, the coverage table counts the statements, the statements that have a test, and the tests that pass. The tests run when the page loads. The page draws the table again when a test finishes and when a cell of this page is added, edited or deleted. A test that has not finished is counted as running.`
// %%
md`**Related work.** The static rules for local writes (section 4) use the structure of reference immutability with uniqueness: Gordon, Parkinson, Parsons, Bromfield and Duffy, *Uniqueness and Reference Immutability for Safe Parallelism*, OOPSLA 2012 ([extended version, MSR-TR-2012-79](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/wp-content/uploads/2016/02/msr-tr-2012-79.pdf)). The static rules alone are not sound. The guarantee depends on the freeze at run time (S4.6).

- That system has four permissions on a reference: writable, readable, immutable and isolated. In SoundScript \`Mut<T>\` is writable at its first level only (S4.7). In the paper, writable applies at every level. Inside its cell, a plain \`T\` that is another name for a \`Mut\` (S4.4) is readable in the sense of the paper: it cannot write, and another name can. After a value is published, a plain \`T\` is immutable. There is no isolated permission.
- The recovery rule of the paper applies when everything that a block reads is immutable or isolated. A writable value that the block produces then has no alias outside the block. Thus it can be isolated and then immutable, and aliases inside the block need no tracking. A cell reads only frozen values, which is the condition of that rule.
- The rule needs a language with no mutable global state. It says nothing about code that runs after the block has given its value. The formal language of the paper has no closures, arrays or exceptions. Its model of parallelism is two blocks that both end, with no task that is joined later. In SoundScript, the code that runs later is a kept function, a generator after a \`yield\`, and an async function after an \`await\`. S4.5, S4.6 and S4.8 apply to them.
- The paper describes no run-time check. SoundScript freezes each value when it is published (S4.6). That freeze stops a write that the static rules do not find.`
// %%
spec_coverage_view = {
  const rows = spec_coverage.rows.map((r) => r.statements === 0
    ? htl.html`<tr><td>${r.section}</td><td>${r.title}</td><td colspan="4">no statements yet</td></tr>`
    : htl.html`<tr><td>${r.section}</td><td>${r.title}</td><td>${r.statements}</td><td>${r.tested}</td><td>${r.passing}/${r.tests}${r.running ? ` (${r.running} running)` : ""}</td><td>${r.untested.join(", ")}</td></tr>`);
  const unknown = spec_coverage.unknown.length ? htl.html`<p>Tests naming a statement that does not exist: ${spec_coverage.unknown.join(", ")}</p>` : "";
  return htl.html`<table><thead><tr><th>§</th><th>section</th><th>statements</th><th>with a test</th><th>tests passing</th><th>statements with no test</th></tr></thead><tbody>${rows}</tbody></table>${unknown}`;
}
// %%
md`## 1. Cells and the top level

A module is a list of cells. A cell is a named function at the top level. All other top-level declarations are types.`
// %%
md`**S1.1** A cell is a top-level \`function\`, \`function*\`, \`async function\` or \`async function*\` declaration. A cell is also one declarator of a top-level \`const\` or \`let\` whose initializer is an arrow or a function expression. The declared name is the name of the cell.`
// %%
md`**S1.2** \`export\` before a cell or a type declaration is accepted and changes nothing.`
// %%
md`**S1.3** The top level can also hold \`type\` and \`interface\` declarations and \`import type\`. They are not cells.`
// %%
md`**S1.4** Any other top-level statement is \`[module-level]\`. The exceptions are \`var\`, \`class\`, \`enum\` and \`namespace\`, which are \`[no-var]\`, \`[no-class]\`, \`[no-enum]\` and \`[no-namespace]\` (section 3).`
// %%
md`**S1.5** A top-level \`const\` or \`let\` that is not a function is \`[cell-not-function]\`. This includes a declaration with no initializer and a declaration that destructures.`
// %%
md`**S1.6** A name that more than one cell declares belongs to the first cell. Each later cell with that name is \`[duplicate-cell]\` ("a is already defined at line 1"). The checker reports the later cells as \`a (2)\`, \`a (3)\` and so on. The checker checks the first cell as usual, and a cell that takes the name reads the first cell. In a notebook module, an edit that gives a cell the name of another cell is stored as \`a (2)\` and does not run. The compiled variable of \`a (2)\` has the name \`$ss$2$a\`. A type declaration \`type X\` is the variable \`$ss$type$X\`. Thus each has a legal Observable JavaScript name.`
// %%
md`**S1.7** A cell with the name of a shell cell is \`[duplicate-cell]\`. A shell cell is a cell that is not checked, which the checker gets together with the checked cells (section 9). A notebook module gives the checker no shell cells.`
// %%
md`**S1.8** One \`const\` or \`let\` statement can declare several cells: \`const a = () => 1, b = () => 2;\` is two cells.`
// %%
md`**S1.9** A cell that takes itself is \`[cycle]\`. A cell that takes a cell that takes it back, directly or through other cells, is also \`[cycle]\` ("dependency cycle a -> b -> a"). The checker rejects every cell on the cycle.`
// %%
md`**S1.10** A top-level \`type\` or \`interface\` cannot have the name of a type of the checker. Such a declaration is \`[builtin-type-name]\`. The names are \`Array ReadonlyArray Record Js ValueOf String Number Boolean RegExp RegExpMatchArray Map Set Error Promise ArrayBuffer URL URLSearchParams Float32Array Float64Array Int8Array Uint8Array Uint8ClampedArray Int16Array Uint16Array Int32Array Uint32Array Mut Borrowed State StateCell Action Events Listener Generator Iterable ArrayLike Clock Random Fetch FetchResponse Log Invalidation Compile Pool Cast Elem Child PointerData CommonProps ButtonProps InputProps CanvasProps IntrinsicElements\`. The declaration is not loaded, and the cells of the module read the type of the checker with that name.

Since 2026-11-23, a type alias that is defined by itself is \`[type-cycle]\`, and is not loaded. An alias is defined by itself when no object, array, tuple or function type is between the alias and its own name. Names, unions, intersections, parentheses and generic aliases that return an argument do not count as between: \`type Key = Key\`, \`type A = B | number; type B = A\`. A use of such an alias adds no error of its own, so the program has one \`[type-cycle]\` for each alias (since 2026-11-23, a9; before that, each use was also \`[unknown-type]\`). \`type Tree = { readonly kids: readonly Tree[] }\` is accepted.`
// %%
md`These are the cells of this section, in SoundScript. \`s1_exported\` keeps its \`export\` (S1.2), and the editor marks it "export is not needed".`
// %%
//ss: ./spec.ts
// %%
md`Rejected sources. The page checks each source when it loads. The fourth column is the report of the checker. The last column tells you if that report is the given rule.`
// %%
spec_rejects_1 = [
  { statement: "S1.4", rule: "module-level", source: "for (;;) {}\nlet a = () => 1;" },
  { statement: "S1.4", rule: "module-level", source: "1 + 1;" },
  { statement: "S1.4", rule: "no-class", source: "export class A {}\nconst a = () => 1;" },
  { statement: "S1.4", rule: "no-enum", source: "export enum E { a }\nconst a = () => 1;" },
  { statement: "S1.5", rule: "cell-not-function", source: "let a = () => 1;\nlet x = 42;" },
  { statement: "S1.5", rule: "cell-not-function", source: "let x;" },
  { statement: "S1.5", rule: "cell-not-function", source: "const { a } = { a: () => 1 };" },
  { statement: "S1.6", rule: "duplicate-cell", source: "const a = () => 1;\nconst a = () => 2;" },
  { statement: "S1.7", rule: "duplicate-cell", source: "let a = () => 1;", shell: "export let a = () => 2;" },
  { statement: "S1.9", rule: "cycle", source: "const a = (a: number) => a + 1;" },
  { statement: "S1.9", rule: "cycle", source: "const a = (b: number) => b + 1;\nconst b = (a: number) => a + 1;" },
  { statement: "S1.10", rule: "builtin-type-name", source: "type Listener = { readonly n: number };" },
  { statement: "S1.10", rule: "builtin-type-name", source: "interface Array<T> { readonly length: number; push(x: T): number }" },
  { statement: "S1.10", rule: "builtin-type-name", source: "type Pool = { readonly size: number };\nconst a = (pool: Pool) => pool.size;" },
  { statement: "S1.10", rule: "type-cycle", source: "type Key = Key;" },
  { statement: "S1.10", rule: "type-cycle", source: "type A = B | number;\ntype B = A;" },
  { statement: "S1.10", rule: "type-cycle", source: "type Box<T> = T;\ntype K = Box<K>;" }
]
// %%
spec_rejects_1_view = spec_rejectsTable(spec_rejects_1)
// %%
spec_rejects = [...spec_rejects_1, ...spec_rejects_2, ...spec_rejects_3, ...spec_rejects_4, ...spec_rejects_5, ...spec_rejects_6, ...spec_rejects_7, ...spec_rejects_9, ...spec_rejects_10, ...spec_rejects_11]
// %%
spec_rulesOf = (c) => {
  // the rule ids the checker reports for a reject case, sorted and without repeats. outside: the cells in
  // other languages the case's module has (section 11)
  const r = soundscript.checkSources({ cells: c.source, shell: c.shell, tsx: true, outside: c.outside });
  return [...new Set([...r.module, ...r.cells.flatMap((x) => x.errors)].map((e) => e.rule))].sort();
}
// %%
spec_rejectsTable = (cases) => htl.html`<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>source</th><th>rule</th><th>checker</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody>${cases.map((c) => {
  const got = spec_rulesOf(c);
  const same = got.length === 1 && got[0] === c.rule;
  const notes = [c.shell ? "// shell: " + c.shell : "", c.outside ? "// other languages: " + (c.outside.map((o) => (o.view ? "viewof " : "") + o.name).join(", ") || "none") : ""].filter(Boolean);
  return htl.html`<tr><td>${c.statement}</td><td><pre style="margin:0">${[c.source, ...notes].join("\n")}</pre></td><td>[${c.rule}]</td><td>${got.map((r) => `[${r}]`).join(" ")}</td><td>${same ? "same" : "DIFFERENT"}</td></tr>`;
})}</tbody></table>`
// %%
spec_rejectsFor = (statement) => {
  // every reject case of a statement gives exactly its rule
  const cases = spec_rejects.filter((c) => c.statement === statement);
  if (!cases.length) throw new Error(`${statement} has no reject case`);
  for (const c of cases) {
    const got = spec_rulesOf(c);
    if (got.length !== 1 || got[0] !== c.rule) throw new Error(`${JSON.stringify(c.source)} gave ${JSON.stringify(got)}, expected exactly [${c.rule}]`);
  }
  return `${cases.length} source(s) give exactly [${[...new Set(cases.map((c) => c.rule))].join(", ")}]`;
}
// %%
// sources a deleted ownership rule rejected that are accepted since the 2026-11-22 language change (S4): a cell's value
// is frozen when it leaves the cell, so what another cell holds cannot change; and (2026-11-23, e1) writes on a receiver
// made in the same expression (S4.3). was: the rule that rejected it
spec_accepts = [
  { statement: "S3.1", was: "member-write", source: "const w = () => {\n  const l = { a: 1 };\n  l.a = 2;\n  return l;\n};" },
  { statement: "S3.11", was: "let-capture", source: "const passed = () => {\n  let n = 0;\n  [1, 2].forEach((x) => {\n    n += x;\n  });\n  return n;\n};" },
  { statement: "S3.18", was: "mut-escape", source: "const f = () => (a: number) => {\n  const m = [1];\n  switch (a) {\n    case 1: return m;\n  }\n  return m;\n};" },
  { statement: "S3.18", was: "mut-after-freeze", source: "const f = () => (a: number): readonly number[] => {\n  const m = [1];\n  let out: readonly number[] = [];\n  switch (a) {\n    case 1: out = m; break;\n  }\n  m.push(2);\n  return out;\n};" },
  { statement: "S3.18", was: "mut-after-freeze", source: "const f = () => (xs: readonly number[]): readonly number[] => {\n  const m = [1];\n  let out: readonly number[] = [];\n  for (const x of xs) {\n    switch (x) {\n      case 1: out = m; continue;\n    }\n    m.push(x);\n  }\n  return out;\n};" },
  { statement: "S3.18", was: "let-capture", source: "const f = () => (a: number): number => {\n  let n = 0;\n  switch (a) {\n    case 1: return [1].map((x) => { n = x; return x; }).length;\n  }\n  return n;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.2", was: "mut-bind", source: "const a = () => {\n  let out = [0];\n  out[0] = 1;\n  return out;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.2", was: "mut-bind", source: "const a = () => [0];" },
  { statement: "S4.2", was: "mut-bind", source: "const a = () => [[0]];" },
  { statement: "S4.2", was: "mut-bind", source: "const a = () => {\n  const g = { h: { t: [1] } };\n  return 1;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.2", was: "mut-field-assign", source: "const a = () => {\n  const g = { t: [1] };\n  g.t = [2];\n  return 1;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.3", was: "mut-not-fresh", source: "const bad = () => {\n  const a = [1];\n  const f = a;\n  const b = f;\n  b[0] = 2;\n  return b;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.4", was: "mut-escape", source: "const sum = () => (xs: number[]) => xs.reduce((p, q) => p + q, 0);\nconst a = (sum) => {\n  const out = [0, 1];\n  const alias = out;\n  return 1;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.4", was: "mut-escape", source: "const sum = () => (xs: number[]) => xs.reduce((p, q) => p + q, 0);\nconst a = (sum) => {\n  const out = [0, 1];\n  const arr = [out];\n  return 1;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.4", was: "mut-escape", source: "const sum = () => (xs: number[]) => xs.reduce((p, q) => p + q, 0);\nconst a = (sum) => {\n  const out = [0, 1];\n  sum(out);\n  return 1;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.4", was: "mut-escape", source: "const a = () => {\n  const out = [0, 1];\n  return out;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.4", was: "mut-escape", source: "const b = () => {\n  const out = [0];\n  return { out };\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.4", was: "mut-escape", source: "const g = function* () {\n  const out = [0, 1];\n  yield out;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.4", was: "mut-escape", source: "const a = () => {\n  const g = { t: [1] };\n  const t = g.t;\n  return g.t.length;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.4", was: "mut-escape", source: "const a = () => {\n  const g = { t: [1] };\n  const h = { ...g };\n  return g.t.length;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.5", was: "mut-capture", source: "const a = () => {\n  const out = [0];\n  [1].forEach(() => {\n    out[0] = 1;\n  });\n  return out;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.6", was: "mut-after-freeze", source: "const a = () => {\n  const out = [0];\n  const r = out;\n  out[0] = 2;\n  return r;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.6", was: "mut-after-freeze", source: "const a = () => {\n  const out = [0];\n  const f = () => {\n    out[0] = 1;\n  };\n  const r = out;\n  f();\n  return r;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.8", was: "mut-view", source: "const a = () => {\n  const t = new Float32Array(4);\n  const v = t.subarray(0, 2);\n  return t;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.8", was: "mut-view", source: "const a = () => {\n  const m = new Map<string, number>();\n  const set = (k: string) => m.set(k, 1);\n  set(\"a\");\n  return m.size;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.9", was: "mut-freeze-param", source: "const a = () => {\n  const g = (m: Mut<number[]>) => m;\n  return 1;\n};" },
  { statement: "S5.1", was: "borrow-placement", source: "const a = () => {\n  const b = [1];\n  return snapshot(b);\n};" },
  { statement: "S5.1", was: "borrow-placement", source: "const g = function* () {\n  const b = [1];\n  const f = function* () {\n    yield b;\n  };\n  yield 1;\n};" },
  { statement: "S5.2", was: "inject-type", source: "const frames = function* () {\n  for (let k = 0; k < 2; k++) {\n    const buf = new Float32Array(2);\n    buf[0] = k;\n    yield buf.slice();\n  }\n};\nconst plain = (frames: Float32Array) => frames.length;" },
  { statement: "S5.3", was: "borrowed-escape", source: "const frames = function* () {\n  for (let k = 0; k < 2; k++) {\n    const buf = new Float32Array(2);\n    buf[0] = k;\n    yield buf.slice();\n  }\n};\nconst bad = (frames) => frames;" },
  { statement: "S5.3", was: "borrowed-escape", source: "const frames = function* () {\n  for (let k = 0; k < 2; k++) {\n    const buf = new Float32Array(2);\n    buf[0] = k;\n    yield buf.slice();\n  }\n};\nconst bad = (frames) => {\n  const f = () => frames.length;\n  return f();\n};" },
  { statement: "S5.3", was: "borrowed-escape", source: "const frames = function* () {\n  for (let k = 0; k < 2; k++) {\n    const buf = new Float32Array(2);\n    buf[0] = k;\n    yield buf.slice();\n  }\n};\nconst bad = (frames) => [frames];" },
  { statement: "S5.3", was: "borrowed-escape", source: "const frames = function* () {\n  for (let k = 0; k < 2; k++) {\n    const buf = new Float32Array(2);\n    buf[0] = k;\n    yield buf.slice();\n  }\n};\nconst bad = (frames) => state(frames);" },
  { statement: "S5.3", was: "borrowed-escape", source: "const frames = function* () {\n  for (let k = 0; k < 2; k++) {\n    const buf = new Float32Array(2);\n    buf[0] = k;\n    yield buf.slice();\n  }\n};\nconst bad = (frames) => {\n  let x = frames;\n  return 1;\n};" },
  { statement: "S5.3", was: "borrowed-escape", source: "const frames = function* () {\n  for (let k = 0; k < 2; k++) {\n    const buf = new Float32Array(2);\n    buf[0] = k;\n    yield buf.slice();\n  }\n};\nconst bad = function* (frames) {\n  yield frames;\n};" },
  { statement: "S5.4", was: "borrowed-await", source: "const frames = function* () {\n  for (let k = 0; k < 2; k++) {\n    const buf = new Float32Array(2);\n    buf[0] = k;\n    yield buf.slice();\n  }\n};\nconst bad = async (frames) => {\n  await Promise.resolve();\n  return frames.length;\n};" },
  { statement: "S5.5", was: "type", source: "const f = () => snapshot([1]);" },
  { statement: "S2.11", was: "type", source: "const f = () => {\n  const g = async () => Promise.resolve(5);\n  return 1;\n};" },
  { statement: "S2.11", was: "type", source: "const f = async () => {\n  async function g(): Promise<number> {\n    return Promise.resolve(5);\n  }\n  const n: number = await g();\n  return n;\n};" },
  { statement: "S7.1", was: "inject-events", source: "const keys = function* (now: Clock) {\n  yield { key: \"a\", t: now() };\n};\nconst onKey = () => action((e: { readonly key: string; readonly t: number }) => {});\nconst l = (keys: Events<{ readonly key: string; readonly t: number }>, onKey: Action<[{ readonly key: string; readonly t: number }]>) => on(keys, onKey);" },
  { statement: "S4.3", was: "member-write", source: "const a = () => {\n  const m: { [k: string]: number } = {};\n  m[\"a\"] = 1;\n  return m;\n};" },
  { statement: "S6.20", was: "excess-property", source: "const B = () => (p: { readonly n: number }) => <span>{p.n}</span>;\nconst v = (B: (p: { readonly n: number }) => Elem) => <div>{[1, 2].map((n) => <B key={n} n={n} />)}</div>;" },
  { statement: "S4.3", was: "mutating-method", source: "const xs = () => [3, 1];\nconst b = (xs: readonly number[]) => [...xs].sort();" },
  { statement: "S4.3", was: "mutating-method", source: "const xs = () => [3, 1];\nconst b = (xs: readonly number[]) => xs.slice().reverse();" },
  { statement: "S4.3", was: "member-write", source: "const xs = () => [3, 1];\nconst b = (xs: readonly number[]) => {\n  const ys = [...xs];\n  [...xs][0] = 9;\n  return ys;\n};" },
  { statement: "S4.3", was: "mutating-method", source: "const xs = () => [3, 1];\nconst b = (xs: readonly number[]) => xs.map((x) => x * 2).sort();" },
  { statement: "S4.3", was: "mutating-method", source: "const m = () => new Map<string, number>();\nconst b = (m: Map<string, number>) => new Map(m).set(\"a\", 1).size;" }
]
// %%
spec_acceptsFor = (statement) => {
  const cases = spec_accepts.filter((c) => c.statement === statement);
  if (!cases.length) throw new Error(`${statement} has no accepted case`);
  for (const c of cases) spec_okCells(c.source);
  return `${cases.length} source(s) once rejected by [${[...new Set(cases.map((c) => c.was))].join(", ")}] are accepted`;
}
// %%
spec_okCells = (source) => {
  // the cells of an accepted source, name:status; throws if the source is rejected
  const r = soundscript.check(source);
  const rules = [...r.module, ...r.cells.flatMap((x) => x.errors)].map((e) => e.rule);
  if (rules.length) throw new Error(`${JSON.stringify(source)} gave ${JSON.stringify(rules)}`);
  return r.cells.map((c) => `${c.name}:${c.status}`).join(",");
}
// %%
spec_is = (got, want) => {
  // throws unless got and want are the same JSON
  if (JSON.stringify(got) !== JSON.stringify(want)) throw new Error(`gave ${JSON.stringify(got)}, expected ${JSON.stringify(want)}`);
  return got;
}
// %%
spec_flush = () => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 0)).then(() => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 0)))
// %%
spec_frames = async (f, n = 600) => {
  // animation frames until f() resolves true, at most n; the last f()
  for (let i = 0; i < n && !(await f()); i++) await new Promise((r) => requestAnimationFrame(r));
  return f();
}
// %%
spec_runs = {
  // computations of a variable the runtime started since spec_runs was first asked about it: the pending calls
  // of an observer (runtime-sdk observe), one per computation. observe also calls pending once on attaching, in
  // a microtask it queues then; the first answer waits for it and does not count it.
  const counts = new WeakMap();
  return async (v) => {
    let c = counts.get(v);
    if (!c) {
      c = { n: 0 };
      counts.set(v, c);
      observe(v, { pending: () => c.n++ });
      await null;
      c.n = 0;
    }
    return c.n;
  };
}
// %%
spec_evals = (module, name) => {
  // runs of a cell (spec_runs); a state cell runs in its handle variable
  const v = (n) => [...runtime._variables].find((x) => x._module === module && x._name === n);
  return spec_runs(v(soundscript.handleName(name)) ?? v(name));
}
// %%
spec_editor = async (texts) => {
  // a module of its own that hosts SoundScript (it has ss_rejected), whose cells are applied and edited as the editor does (ss_language.apply);
  // value(n) is the value, or { error } with the message it raises; dispose deletes every variable
  const m = runtime.module();
  m.define(soundscript.INPUTS.rejected, [], () => soundscript.rejected);
  const at = { module: { module: m } };
  for (const t of texts) await ss_language.apply(t, [], at);
  const variable = (n) => m._scope.get(n);
  return {
    module: m,
    at,
    variable,
    edit: (name, text) => ss_language.apply(text, [variable(name)], at),
    states: () => Object.fromEntries([...ss_check(m).states].map(([n, x]) => [n, x.state === "blocked" ? `blocked by ${x.blockedBy.join(",")}` : x.state])),
    value: async (n) => { try { return await m.value(n); } catch (e) { return { error: e.message }; } },
    evals: (n) => spec_runs(variable(soundscript.handleName(n)) ?? variable(n)),
    text: (n) => soundscript.decompileVariable(String(variable(n)._definition)),
    dispose: () => { for (const v of [...runtime._variables]) if (v._module === m) { soundscript.release(v._value); v.delete(); } }
  };
}
// %%
spec_okCellsWith = (source, outside) => {
  // spec_okCells for a module that also has cells in other languages
  const r = soundscript.checkSources({ cells: source, tsx: true, outside });
  const rules = [...r.module, ...r.cells.flatMap((x) => x.errors)].map((e) => e.rule);
  if (rules.length) throw new Error(`${JSON.stringify(source)} gave ${JSON.stringify(rules)}`);
  return r.cells.map((c) => `${c.name}:${c.status}`).join(",");
}
// %%
test_spec_S1_1 = {
  const forms = "const a = () => 1;\nlet b = (a: number) => a + 1;\nconst g = function (a: number) { return a; };\nfunction c(b: number) { return b * 2; }\nfunction* d() { yield 1; }\nasync function e() { return 1; }\nasync function* f() { yield 1; }";
  const got = spec_okCells(forms);
  if (got !== "a:ok,b:ok,g:ok,c:ok,d:ok,e:ok,f:ok") throw new Error(got);
  const values = await Promise.all(["s1_arrow", "s1_let", "s1_declared", "s1_generator", "s1_async"].map((n) => spec_module.value(n)));
  if (JSON.stringify(values) !== "[1,2,4,1,4]") throw new Error(`the live cells gave ${JSON.stringify(values)}`);
  return `7 forms check as cells; the live cells give ${JSON.stringify(values)}`;
}
// %%
test_spec_S1_2 = {
  const plain = "const a = () => 1;\nfunction c(a: number) { return a; }\ntype T = number;\ninterface I { x: number }";
  const exported = plain.replace(/^/gm, "export ");
  const a = spec_okCells(plain), b = spec_okCells(exported);
  if (a !== b) throw new Error(`${a} without export, ${b} with`);
  if ((await spec_module.value("s1_exported")) !== 5) throw new Error("s1_exported is not 5");
  return `the same cells with and without export (${a}); s1_exported is 5`;
}
// %%
test_spec_S1_3 = {
  const got = spec_okCells('type T = number;\ninterface I { x: number }\nimport type { X } from "./x";\nconst a = (): T => 1;');
  if (got !== "a:ok") throw new Error(got);
  if (JSON.stringify(await spec_module.value("s1_typed")) !== '{"a":1,"b":2}') throw new Error("s1_typed");
  return "type, interface and import type are accepted and are not cells";
}
// %%
test_spec_S1_4 = {
  const own = [["var a = () => 1;", "no-var"], ["class A {}", "no-class"], ["enum E { A }", "no-enum"], ["namespace N {}", "no-namespace"]];
  for (const [source, rule] of own) {
    const got = spec_rulesOf({ source });
    if (got.length !== 1 || got[0] !== rule) throw new Error(`${source} gave ${JSON.stringify(got)}, expected [${rule}]`);
  }
  return `${spec_rejectsFor("S1.4")}; var, class, enum and namespace give their own rules`;
}
// %%
test_spec_S1_5 = spec_rejectsFor("S1.5")
// %%
test_spec_S1_6 = {
  const r = soundscript.check('const a = () => 1;\nconst b = (a: number) => a + 1;\nconst a = () => "x";\nconst a = () => 3;');
  const got = r.cells.map((c) => `${c.name}:${c.status}:${c.errors.map((e) => e.msg).join("|")}`).join(",");
  if (got !== "a:ok:,b:ok:,a (2):rejected:a is already defined at line 1,a (3):rejected:a is already defined at line 1") throw new Error(got);
  if (r.cells[1].value !== "number") throw new Error(`b reads ${r.cells[1].value}, not the first a`);
  // an edit naming another cell is stored, rejected, under the next free name
  const e = ss_language.check("const s1_arrow = () => 9;", [], { module: { module: spec_module } });
  if (!e.store || e.state !== "rejected" || e.defs.map((d) => soundscript.keyOfVariable(d.name)).join() !== "s1_arrow (2)" || !e.problems.some((p) => p.rule === "duplicate-cell")) throw new Error(`the edit gave store ${e.store}, ${e.state}, ${e.defs?.map((d) => d.name)}, ${JSON.stringify(e.problems)}`);
  return `${spec_rejectsFor("S1.6")}; b reads the first a; an edit naming s1_arrow is stored as s1_arrow (2), rejected`;
}
// %%
test_spec_S1_6_first_deleted = {
  // a module of its own, edited as the editor edits: a second dupa is stored as dupa (2); once the first is
  // deleted, the module is checked again and dupa (2) becomes dupa
  const m = runtime.module();
  m.define(soundscript.INPUTS.rejected, [], () => soundscript.rejected);
  const at = { module: { module: m } };
  const named = () => [...runtime._variables].filter((v) => v._module === m && typeof v._name === "string" && soundscript.keyOfVariable(v._name).startsWith("dupa")).map((v) => soundscript.keyOfVariable(v._name)).sort().join(",");
  const until = async (f) => { for (let i = 0; i < 200 && !f(); i++) await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 50)); return f(); };
  try {
    await ss_language.apply("const dupa = () => 1;", [], at);
    await ss_language.apply("const dupa = () => 2;", [], at);
    if (named() !== "dupa,dupa (2)") throw new Error(`after two edits: ${named()}`);
    m._scope.get("dupa").delete();
    if (!(await until(() => named() === "dupa"))) throw new Error(`after deleting the first: ${named()}`);
    if ((await m.value("dupa")) !== 2) throw new Error("dupa is not the second cell's value");
    return "dupa (2) became dupa, with value 2, once the first dupa was deleted";
  } finally { for (const v of [...runtime._variables]) if (v._module === m) v.delete(); }
}
// %%
test_spec_S1_7 = spec_rejectsFor("S1.7")
// %%
test_spec_S1_8 = {
  const got = spec_okCells("const a = () => 1, b = (a: number) => a + 1;");
  if (got !== "a:ok,b:ok") throw new Error(got);
  const L = await spec_load("const a = () => 1, b = (a: number) => a + 1;", {});
  try { spec_is(await L.value("b"), 2); } finally { L.dispose(); }
  return "one statement, two cells; both run (b is 2)";
}
// %%
test_spec_S1_10 = {
  // the names are read from S1.10's own text; each, declared as a type, is rejected
  const statement = [...runtime._variables].filter((v) => v._module === spec_module).map((v) => String(v._definition)).find((d) => d.includes("**S1.10**"));
  const names = /The names are \\`([^`\\]+)\\`/.exec(statement)[1].split(" ");
  spec_is([...names].sort(), soundscript.builtinTypeNames());
  const bad = names.filter((n) => JSON.stringify(spec_rulesOf({ source: `type ${n} = { readonly n: number };` })) !== '["builtin-type-name"]');
  if (bad.length) throw new Error(`not rejected: ${bad.join(", ")}`);
  // the cells read the built-in: a user Array without @mutates does not let push through, a user Pool does not retype the capability
  spec_is(spec_rulesOf({ source: "interface Array<T> { push(x: T): number }\nconst xs = () => [1];\nconst w = (xs) => xs.push(1);" }), ["builtin-type-name", "mutating-method"]);
  spec_is(spec_rulesOf({ source: "type Pool = { readonly m: number };\nconst a = (pool: Pool) => pool.m;" }), ["builtin-type-name", "no-member"]);
  spec_is(spec_okCells("type ListenerOf = { readonly n: number };\nconst one = () => (l: ListenerOf): number => l.n;"), "one:ok");
  return `${spec_rejectsFor("S1.10")}; the list is the checker's, all ${names.length} names rejected; the cells read the built-in; another name is accepted`;
}
// %%
test_spec_S1_9 = {
  const st = soundscript.check("const a = (b: number) => b + 1;\nconst b = (a: number) => a + 1;").cells.map((c) => `${c.name}:${c.status}`).join(",");
  spec_is(st, "a:rejected,b:rejected");
  return `${spec_rejectsFor("S1.9")}; both cells of a two-cell cycle are rejected`;
}
// %%
md`## 2. Inputs and names

The parameters of a cell name the cells that it reads. A body can use its parameters, its locals, a fixed set of prelude functions and intrinsics, and the capabilities that it takes as parameters. A body can use no other name.`
// %%
md`**S2.1** The parameters of a cell are its inputs. The cell takes them by name. A parameter must name a cell of the module, a foreign declaration, a shell cell, a capability, or a variable in another language (section 11). If it does not, it is \`[inject-unknown]\`. Only the parameter list of the cell itself is its input list. Thus a cell whose value is a function is two arrows: \`const double = () => (n: number) => n * 2\`.`
// %%
md`**S2.2** An input is a plain identifier. A destructuring pattern, a default value or a rest parameter is \`[inject-pattern]\`.`
// %%
md`**S2.3** An input with no type annotation has the value type of the cell that it names. An input with an annotation must accept that value type. If it does not, it is \`[inject-type]\`.`
// %%
md`**S2.4** The value type of a cell is its return type \`T\`, with these exceptions:

- \`Promise<T>\` gives \`T\`;
- a generator or an async generator that yields \`Y\` gives \`Y\`;
- \`StateCell<T>\` gives \`T\`.

An input annotated with the wrapped type, such as \`Promise<number>\`, is \`[inject-type]\`.

The runtime awaits the value of a cell. Thus, since 2026-11-23 (a9), the value type has no \`Promise\` at any level, in each member of a union. \`Promise<Promise<number>>\` gives \`number\`. The same applies to a cell that is not async and returns \`Promise.resolve(5)\`, to a generator that yields a promise, and to a state cell that holds a promise. A promise inside a record or an array is not awaited and keeps its type. A \`State<T>\` or \`Events<T>\` input keeps the promise: \`read\` and the action of a listener get the promise itself.`
// %%
md`**S2.5** The capabilities are the inputs \`now: Clock\`, \`random: Random\`, \`fetch: Fetch\`, \`log: Log\`, \`invalidation: Invalidation\`, \`compile: Compile\` and \`pool: Pool\`. \`Clock\` and \`Random\` are \`() => number\`. Any other annotation on a capability, such as \`now: number\`, is \`[inject-type]\`. The value of \`pool\` is not part of the core. A module with a cell that takes \`pool\` imports \`ss_pool\` from the add-on \`@tomlarkworthy/soundscript-workers\`.`
// %%
md`**S2.6** A body can name:

- its parameters and its own locals;
- the prelude functions \`state action mut freeze borrow snapshot equals on read messageOf memo cast\`;
- the intrinsics \`Object Array Math JSON Number String Boolean BigInt Map Set Error TypeError RangeError RegExp Promise ArrayBuffer URL URLSearchParams Infinity NaN undefined isNaN isFinite parseInt parseFloat encodeURIComponent decodeURIComponent encodeURI decodeURI atob btoa structuredClone Float32Array Float64Array Int8Array Uint8Array Uint8ClampedArray Int16Array Uint16Array Int32Array Uint32Array\`.

Any other free name is \`[free-name]\`, for example \`Date\`, \`console\`, \`window\`, \`globalThis\`, \`setTimeout\`, \`eval\`, \`Function\`.

A call of \`structuredClone\` is \`[type]\` when the type of its argument is or can hold a function or a symbol, or is \`unknown\`, \`object\` or a type parameter. Such a call would throw.

A body can only call a prelude function. The name of a prelude function used as a value is \`[prelude-value]\`. There are two exceptions: \`read\` is \`[read-outside-action]\` (S6.8), and \`on\` is \`[listener-placement]\` (S7.3). A local or a parameter with the name of a prelude function is \`[prelude-shadow]\`. \`mut\`, \`freeze\` and \`borrow\` are in the list so that a call of one, or its name as a value, is \`[removed-call]\` (S4.2).

Since 2026-11-24 (w1), a \`const\` or \`let\` of a block is in scope for the full block, as in JavaScript. A read of its name before the declaration names that declaration and not a binding outside the block. This applies in the block and in a function made in the block. Such a read is \`[free-name]\`. For example, a later local \`Math\` makes an earlier \`Math.max\` in the block \`[free-name]\`. This rule is stricter than JavaScript. A function made in the block that reads a later \`const\` is \`[free-name]\`, even when the function is called only after the declaration.`
// %%
md`**S2.7** A read of \`Math.random\`, \`Object.defineProperty\`, \`Object.defineProperties\`, \`Object.setPrototypeOf\`, or \`prototype\` on \`Object\` or \`Array\` is \`[banned-member]\`. This also applies through a local bound to the intrinsic, through destructuring such as \`const { random } = Math\`, and through a computed key such as \`Math["random"]\`. A value with a banned member is not assignable to a type that names the member. Thus \`Math\` given where \`{ random: () => number }\` is expected is \`[type]\`. \`prototype\` on any other value is rejected by a different rule, usually \`[no-member]\`.

A \`__proto__\` key in an object literal is \`[banned-member]\`, as a name, a string or a computed literal. The name form and the string form set the prototype. The computed form and the shorthand form make an own property. The checker rejects all forms, so that no form of the key is accepted.

Since 2026-11-24, on a value whose type has an index signature (\`{ [k: string]: T }\`, \`Record<string, T>\`), a key that can be any string is \`[banned-member]\`. This applies to a computed member read or write, a \`delete\` and an \`in\` test. A key that is known only at run time can name an inherited member such as \`toString\` or \`__proto__\`. The key must be a string literal, a union of string literals, or a number. A \`const\` bound to a literal has its literal type. Put entries with run-time keys in a \`Map<string, T>\` (\`m.get(k)\`, \`m.set(k, v)\`, \`m.has(k)\`). \`Object.keys\`, \`Object.values\` and \`Object.entries\` read only own properties and are accepted. \`Object.fromEntries\` with a \`"__proto__"\` key makes an own property and does not change the prototype.

Since 2026-11-24 (w1), some member names are \`[banned-member]\` as a member of a type literal, an alias or an interface, optional or required, and as a key of an object literal. The names are the members of \`Object.prototype\` (\`toString\`, \`valueOf\`, \`hasOwnProperty\`, \`isPrototypeOf\`, \`constructor\` and the others, read from \`Object.prototype\` when the checker loads), \`toJSON\`, \`then\` and \`__proto__\`. A template string, \`+\`, \`==\`, \`String()\`, \`join\`, \`sort\`, \`JSON.stringify\` and \`await\` call the own member of a value that has such a name. A conversion would thus run code of the program. A type or an interface that declares such a member is \`[banned-member]\` one time for the module, and is not loaded. A cell that uses it is \`[unknown-type]\`.`
// %%
md`**S2.8** A body that uses a cell by its name, and does not take it as a parameter, is \`[cell-by-name]\`. This includes a cell that calls itself. A local with the name of a cell is a local.`
// %%
md`**S2.9** The checker type-checks a body with \`null\` and \`undefined\` kept apart from other types. A type error is \`[type]\`. Five checks have rules of their own:

- A parameter of a local function with no annotation and no type from the place where the function is passed is \`[untyped-parameter]\`.
- A call with too few or too many arguments is \`[arity]\`. Since 2026-11-25, a function given a function type that declares fewer parameters than the function has is also \`[arity]\` (the rule on parameters past the declared ones, in this statement).
- A property that the declared type of an object literal does not have is \`[excess-property]\`.
- A value that can be \`undefined\` or \`null\`, used as a number, read from, indexed or called, is \`[possibly-undefined]\`.
- An assignment to a \`const\` local is \`[const-assign]\`.

These rules are from 2026-11-24 (rounds w1, x1 and y1):

- **Record types.** A record type can omit any member of a record that it gets. A value that is not a record (a function, an array, a primitive, an \`Error\`, a \`Map\`, a typed array and so on) is \`[forgets]\` when it gets a record type. This includes \`{}\` and a record of optional members.
- **\`unknown\` and \`object\`.** A value whose own type holds a promise, a generator or an iterator (\`Iterable\`, the result of \`values()\`) is \`[forgets]\` when it gets \`unknown\` or \`object\`. The checker reports this direct case. A function or a \`Mut\` given \`unknown\` or \`object\` is accepted. An \`Action\` given \`unknown\` or \`object\` is \`[action-pass]\` (S6.6), not \`[forgets]\`.
- **\`void\`.** A function result typed \`void\` takes only \`undefined\`. A callback that returns a value where \`(x: T) => void\` is declared is \`[type]\`. A block body that returns nothing is accepted. \`forEach\` declares the result of its callback as \`unknown\`.
- **Call signatures.** Every function has the members of \`Function.prototype\` (\`name\`, \`length\`, \`bind\` and the others, read when the checker runs). A function given a type with a call signature that declares one of these members is \`[forgets]\`, unless the own type of the function declares it.
- **\`JSON.stringify\`.** \`JSON.stringify(v)\` is \`[type]\` when \`v\` can be \`undefined\`, a function, \`unknown\`, \`object\` or a type parameter. \`JSON.stringify\` gives \`undefined\` for those, not a string. \`JSON.stringify(v ?? null)\` is accepted.
- **Parameters past the declared ones** (2026-11-25). A function type says how many arguments a caller passes. JavaScript can pass more. \`map\`, \`filter\`, \`forEach\` and the other array methods pass the element, the index and the array. The checker's signatures leave out the array. A parameter past the declared ones would get that argument, which can have a type other than its annotation. So a function is not assignable to a function type that declares fewer parameters than the function has. This counts optional parameters, parameters with a default value and a rest parameter, unless the type has a rest parameter. The rule applies wherever one function type is compared with another: an annotation, a record member, an array element, a type argument, a union member, a call signature, a cell's parameter and the parameter of a built-in. It is \`[arity]\`, and the message says to remove the parameters after the declared ones, or to declare them in the type. In a cell's parameter it is \`[inject-type]\`. A function with no rest parameter and with fewer parameters than its type declares is accepted.
- **\`Object.fromEntries\`.** The keys of its result come from run-time data. A key can be \`then\`, \`toJSON\` or \`toString\`, which the run time calls. A call whose values are not plain data is \`[type]\`. Values typed \`unknown\` or \`object\` count as not plain, because one can be an \`Action\` that a join made \`unknown\`.

Check time and run time: the rules of this statement are rules of the checker. The run time stops these cases where a value is handed over (S4.6): a response or an iterator that a type omits, and a promise that a type omits in the value of a cell whose type names no promise. This includes a value that reaches \`unknown\` through a join with a value typed \`unknown\` or through a type parameter. A conversion does not reach a function that a type omits (S2.7). If a type incorrectly describes a value as plain data, no run-time check finds it.`
// %%
md`**S2.10** Reserved names. Each use in this statement is \`[reserved-name]\`.

- \`viewof\`, \`mutable\` and \`$\` followed by digits (\`$0\`, \`$12\`) cannot be the name of a cell, a parameter or a local. \`$0\` also cannot be a property key or a member name. \`viewof\` and \`mutable\` can be a property key or a member name, and a type can use any of them. A compiled cell is Observable JavaScript, which reads \`viewof\` and \`mutable\` as keywords and names the \`viewof\` inputs of a cell \`$0\`, \`$1\` and so on.
- Since 2026-11-23, a name that starts with \`ss_\` or \`$ss$\` is reserved in the same places. The compiler gives the runtime cells that a compiled cell takes the names \`ss_<name>\`. It adds calls of \`ss_closure\` and \`ss_methods\` with a list whose parameter is \`ss_i\`. It names a duplicate or a type \`$ss$…\`. A binding with such a name would replace these.
- A cell cannot have the name \`Generators\` or \`invalidation\`. Compiled cells take these from the module. A parameter with the name \`invalidation\` is the capability (S2.5).
- Since 2026-11-24, a cell cannot take the name of a prelude function or \`h\`. The list is \`tcPrelude\` of the toolchain: \`state\`, \`action\`, \`snapshot\`, \`equals\`, \`on\`, \`read\`, \`messageOf\`, \`memo\`, \`cast\` and \`h\`. A compiled cell that uses one of them binds it from the runtime module, and a cell with that name conflicts with the binding. The runtime module defines these names and is exempt. The checker identifies the runtime module by the identity of the module that it gets, not by the names of its cells. A parameter or a local can take such a name.
- Since 2026-11-24 (t1), a cell cannot take the name of a capability or of a global that a compiled cell takes by name from outside the module. These are each capability (\`now\`, \`random\`, \`fetch\`, \`log\`, \`invalidation\`, \`compile\`, \`pool\`, the list \`tcCaps\` of the compiler), each global that the compiler passes as an input (\`structuredClone\`, \`URLSearchParams\`), and each global that the checker declares (\`Map\`, \`Set\`, \`URL\`, \`JSON\`, \`Math\`, \`Object\`, \`parseInt\` and the others of \`ALLOWED_GLOBALS\`). A compiled cell that reads such a name reads the capability or the global, not the cell. The module run as one script reads the cell. The two runners thus gave different values.
- A cell name that contains \`$\` is reserved. The runners give the handle of a state cell the name \`x$ref\`.
- In a cell that has JSX, a binding with the name \`h\` is reserved: a local, a parameter, a function name or a \`catch\` parameter, in any pattern. Each element is a call \`h(...)\`, which would call the binding.
- Since 2026-11-23 (a9), a cell whose text contains \`/*ss\` or \`/*{lang:"ss"}*/\` is reserved, in a string, a comment or a type. The compiler marks the text that it adds with these characters. In a string, write the text in two parts: \`"/" + "*ss"\`.
- Since 2026-11-24 (w1), a binding (a cell, a local, a parameter, a function name or a \`catch\` parameter) is reserved when the parser does not read its name, alone as an expression, as a name. The TypeScript parser accepts \`const this = …\`, which JavaScript does not parse. The check asks the parser, so it applies to each keyword that the parser knows. A contextual keyword that JavaScript accepts as a name, such as \`async\` or \`of\`, is accepted.

A BigInt literal as a property key (\`{ 1n() {} }\`) is unsupported.`
// %%
md`**S2.11** The return type of an async function is \`Promise<T>\`. The return type of a generator function, sync or async, is \`Generator<Y>\`. Any other annotation is \`[type]\`. Each \`yield\` of a generator annotated \`Generator<Y>\` gives a \`Y\`, and a value of a different type is \`[type]\`. The run time unwraps a promise that an async function returns. Thus, since 2026-11-23, the checker types the return as the value that the promise resolves to, as \`await\` does. The same applies to the result of \`then\` and of \`Promise.resolve\`: a callback that returns a \`Promise<T>\` gives a \`Promise<T>\`.`
// %%
md`**S2.12** \`messageOf(e)\` is the text of a caught value \`e\`. It is the \`message\` of an \`Error\` when the message is a string. If not, it is \`String(e)\`. \`messageOf\` does not throw. If the read of the message or the conversion to a string throws, the result is \`(a thrown value whose text cannot be read)\`. A call that does not have exactly one argument is \`[arity]\`.`
// %%
md`**S2.13** \`memo(f)\` is a function of the type of \`f\`. For an argument list that it has seen, it gives the result that it stored for that list.

- **Key.** A key is the number of arguments and each argument in order. A primitive is compared by \`Object.is\`, so \`0\` and \`-0\` are two keys. Any other value is compared by identity.
- **A call with a new key.** The call deep-freezes its arguments in place and calls \`f\` with them, as a call of an action does. If the caller writes such an argument after the call, the write throws a \`TypeError\` (S4.6). The result is deep-frozen and stored.
- **Calls that are not stored.** A call is not stored when the freeze replaces an argument by a copy, such as a typed array or a \`Map\` made by JavaScript. A call is not stored when its arguments reach a function at any depth, such as a record with a function field given to a parameter \`{ readonly n: number }\` by width. An object of a kind that the walk does not know counts as one that reaches a function. S4.12 does not check such a call, because nothing is stored for it. Such a function can read a \`Mut\` through a read-only name (S4.10) that the caller writes later. A stored result would then be wrong for a later call with the same key. A call of \`f\` that throws stores nothing.
- **While \`f\` computes.** A call of an action, a write to a state cell or a call of \`read\` throws.
- **Form.** \`f\` is a function literal. \`memo\` of any other value is unsupported. In \`const fib = memo((n: number): number => …)\`, \`fib\` is bound before the call is checked. Thus \`f\` can call \`fib\` when its parameters and its result have annotations. Without those annotations the cell is unsupported. A call that does not have exactly one argument is \`[arity]\`.
- **Writes after a call.** Let a cell bind a memoised function with \`const f = memo(…)\`. A \`Mut\` local of a function that is named in an argument of a call of \`f\` is not written after that call. Such a write is \`[mutating-method]\` or \`[member-write]\`. A write in a later turn of a loop around the call is after the call. A call of a helper that writes the local is also after the call. A name inside \`snapshot(…)\` in the argument is not given to the function, because \`snapshot\` makes a copy. The checker does not see a write through a second name for the local. It also does not see a call of a memoised function that the cell takes as an input. Such a write throws at run time.`
// %%
md`**S2.14** \`f\` in \`memo(f)\` computes its result from its arguments only. \`f\` is \`[memo-pure]\` in each of these cases:

- \`f\` is async or a generator.
- A parameter type of \`f\` can be or hold a function. A function can read a capability.
- A parameter type of \`f\` holds a \`Mut\`, \`State\`, \`StateCell\`, \`Action\`, \`Events\`, \`Listener\`, \`Promise\`, \`Generator\`, \`Elem\`, \`FetchResponse\`, \`URLSearchParams\` or \`DataView\`.
- The result type of \`f\` holds a \`Mut\`, a state type, a \`Promise\` or a \`Generator\`, or can hold an iterator by the test of S3.19, such as the result of \`[n].values()\`.
- \`f\` uses a name from outside itself that the body of an action cannot use (S6.9): an input that depends on state, a capability, a parameter of an enclosing function, or a const computed from one of these.
- \`f\` uses a const bound to a \`Mut\` local, such as \`c\` in \`const c: readonly number[] = xs\`. S4.10 lets other functions capture \`c\`, but the cell can write \`xs\` after a result is stored.
- \`f\` uses a capability, which includes \`log\` and \`invalidation\`, or a value that holds a \`State\`, \`Action\`, \`Events\` or \`Listener\`.

A \`let\` or a \`Mut\` that \`f\` captures is \`[let-capture]\` or \`[mut-capture]\` (S4.10). The memoised function can be kept, returned and put in a record, as any function value can.`
// %%
