// %%
md`## 6. State cells and actions

A state cell is a cell that holds state. Only an action writes to a state cell. An event handler, a listener or another action calls an action. Nothing calls an action while a cell is evaluated.`
// %%
md`**S6.1** A state cell is a sync cell that is not a generator and whose one returned expression is \`state(v)\`. Its value type is the type of \`v\` (S2.4). \`state(...)\` in any other position is \`[state-placement]\`: bound to a local, in a conditional, in one of two returns, or in an async or generator cell.`
// %%
md`**S6.2** A state cell with inputs is made again, from its initial value, when an input changes. Until then it keeps the value that was written to it, also when it runs again with the same inputs. An input changes when it is not the same value (\`Object.is\`). A new object with equal content is a change. Thus a state cell whose input is an object that another cell makes starts again each time that cell runs.`
// %%
md`**S6.3** An input that names a state cell, \`c\` or \`c: T\`, gets the value of the state cell. The cell runs again after each commit that wrote the state cell (S6.14). An input \`c: State<T>\` gets the state cell. The cell does not run again when the state cell is written. \`State<T>\` is \`[inject-state]\` on a cell that is not a state cell that holds \`T\`, on a capability, and on a foreign declaration.`
// %%
md`**S6.4** A \`State<T>\` has \`set(v: T)\` and \`update(f: (v: T) => T)\` and no other member. A read of another member is \`[no-member]\`. A value of the wrong type is \`[type]\`.`
// %%
md`**S6.5** \`set\` and \`update\` outside the body of an \`action(...)\` are \`[state-write]\`. This includes a function that the cell returns, and a generator. At run time, a write while no function of an action runs throws.`
// %%
md`**S6.6** \`action(f)\` has the type \`Action<P>\`, where \`P\` is the parameter types of \`f\`. A call of an \`Action\` outside the body of an action is \`[action-call]\`. An \`Action\` can be returned, put in a record or an array, or given to a parameter typed \`Action\`.

An \`Action\` given a type that does not tell that it is an \`Action\` is \`[action-pass]\`. Such a type is \`unknown\`, \`object\`, a type parameter of a function of the module, or a record, array, tuple, union or function type that has one of these at the position of the \`Action\`. This applies to an argument, a spread element, an input, a default value, a local with an annotation, an assignment, a returned value and a thrown value. A type parameter of an intrinsic, such as the \`U\` of \`map\` or the \`T\` of \`Promise.resolve\`, takes the own type of the \`Action\` and is accepted.

Since 2026-11-24 (t1), a record type with no required member and no index or call signature (\`{}\`, an empty interface, \`{ readonly a?: number }\`) hides a value as \`object\` does, because it takes any object. An \`Action\` given that type is \`[action-pass]\`. A record given that type keeps the members that the type declares. Only the other members are hidden.

Since 2026-11-24 (y1), an \`Action\` in a part of a value that its type does not declare is accepted. From w1 until y1 this was \`[forgets]\` (S2.9). No member of the type reaches the \`Action\`, no conversion calls it (S2.7), and a call of it while a cell computes throws.

Check time and run time. At check time, the direct cases are \`[action-call]\` and \`[action-pass]\`. At run time, a guard stops an action that is called while a cell computes, and a call of an \`Action\` returns \`undefined\` (S6.13).

The guard, since 2026-11-24:

- (w1) The body of a compiled cell, and each step of a generator cell, run with the guard that \`runCells\` puts around a cell. An action called while a cell computes, by a function that the checker cannot see into, throws \`action called during a cell evaluation\` in both runners.
- (x1) The guard applies to the full evaluation. An async function written in a SoundScript cell and called with the guard on has the guard on again after each \`await\`. The body of an async generator has it on after each \`await\` and \`yield\`. A callback given to \`then\` or \`finally\` while a cell computes has the guard on each time it runs, also after the cell has finished.
- The guard does not apply to an async function that a cell keeps and that an event handler or a listener calls. It does not apply to an async function from a module in another language after its first \`await\`.`
// %%
md`**S6.7** The content type of a state cell cannot contain \`State\` or \`StateCell\`, directly or in a record, an array, a union, a type argument or a function signature. That is \`[state-in-state]\`.`
// %%
md`**S6.8** Inside an action, \`read(r)\` with \`r: State<T>\` is a \`T\`. It is the stored value with every write made earlier in the call order. This includes a write by an action called before it in the same call. \`read\` outside the body of an action, or used in any way other than a call, is \`[read-outside-action]\`. At run time, \`read\` throws while no function of an action runs.`
// %%
md`**S6.9** The body of an action cannot use a value that depends on state. Such a use is \`[action-capture]\`. A value that depends on state is:

- an input that takes a state cell or an events cell by value;
- a capability other than \`invalidation\` and \`log\`;
- an input that takes a cell that depends on one of these;
- a local computed from any of these.

These are accepted: handles (\`State\`, \`Events\`), \`Action\`s, cells with no state, and the parameters and locals of the action itself. A value from the clock or the random source gets to an action as an argument.`
// %%
md`**S6.10** A write is applied to its call when it is made. Thus an \`update\` whose function throws makes the call that made it throw. A top-level call that throws keeps none of its writes, and none of the writes of the actions that it called. The writes of earlier calls are kept. Calls apply in the order in which they were made. An action called inside a call is a plain function call. If it throws and its caller catches the throw, the writes that it made before the throw are kept.`
// %%
md`**S6.11** A state cell is two runtime variables. \`viewof c\` holds its handle, and \`c\` holds its value. An input \`c: State<T>\` is an input on \`viewof c\` and gets the handle.

- The handle is the same object when the state cell is made again (S6.2). Thus a \`State<T>\` taken before that writes to the new state cell.
- After the state cell was in error, it can have a new handle (S8.5). A cell that takes its \`State<T>\` runs again with the new handle.
- While the state cell is in error, a cell that takes its \`State<T>\` does not run. It is blocked when the state cell is rejected (S8.2). It has the error of the input that threw when the state cell or one of its inputs threw (S8.3).
- When the variables of the state cell are deleted, the holder is rejected with \`[inject-unknown]\` (S2.1) after the module has seen the deletion. Until then it raises the runtime error \`viewof c is not defined\`.
- A function made from the \`State<T>\` before an error and kept outside the dataflow can write to the old handle. No cell reads the old handle.`
// %%
md`**S6.12** The function given to \`action(...)\` is synchronous. These are \`[action-async]\`: an async function, a generator, a function whose return type is or contains a promise-like, and an \`await\` anywhere in its body. To wait for a result, an async cell waits, and a listener gives the result to an action, as in the fetch-then-store example of this section.`
// %%
spec_s6_fetchThenStore = `const asked = (): StateCell<number> => state(0);
const ask = (asked: State<number>) => action(() => asked.update((n) => n + 1));
// the waiting: an async cell, run again for each request
const loaded = async (fetch: Fetch, asked: number) => {
  const r = await fetch(\`https://example.invalid/greeting?n=\${asked}\`);
  return r.ok ? await r.text() : null;
};
// each answer once, as an event
const arrived = function* (loaded: string | null) {
  if (loaded !== null) yield loaded;
};
const greeting = (): StateCell<string> => state("");
const store = (greeting: State<string>) => action((text: string) => greeting.set(text));
const onArrived = (arrived: Events<string>, store: Action<[string]>) => on(arrived, store);`
// %%
md`Fetch, then store. \`ask\` writes a request. \`loaded\` is an async cell that takes the request, waits for the answer, and gives the text. \`arrived\` yields that text one time. \`onArrived\` gives the text to \`store\`, a synchronous action.

\`\`\`ts
${spec_s6_fetchThenStore}
\`\`\``
// %%
spec_s6_latestWins = `type Answer = { readonly n: number; readonly text: string | null };
const asked = (): StateCell<number> => state(0);
const ask = (asked: State<number>) => action(() => asked.update((n) => n + 1));
// the answer carries the request it answers
const loaded = async (fetch: Fetch, asked: number): Promise<Answer> => {
  const r = await fetch(\`https://example.invalid/greeting?n=\${asked}\`);
  return { n: asked, text: r.ok ? await r.text() : null };
};
const arrived = function* (loaded: Answer) {
  yield loaded;
};
const greeting = (): StateCell<string> => state("");
// kept only if it answers the latest request
const store = (greeting: State<string>, asked: State<number>) =>
  action((a: Answer) => {
    if (a.n === read(asked) && a.text !== null) greeting.set(a.text);
  });
const onArrived = (arrived: Events<Answer>, store: Action<[Answer]>) => on(arrived, store);`
// %%
md`Latest wins. An answer to an earlier request still gets to \`store\`. \`store\` compares the request that the answer is for with \`read(asked)\` and discards the answer.

\`\`\`ts
${spec_s6_latestWins}
\`\`\``
// %%
spec_s6_everyAnswer = `type Answer = { readonly n: number; readonly text: string | null };
// the request in flight, and those asked for meanwhile
const current = (): StateCell<number | null> => state<number | null>(0);
const queue = (): StateCell<number[]> => state<number[]>([]);
const issued = (): StateCell<number> => state(1);
const ask = (current: State<number | null>, queue: State<number[]>, issued: State<number>) =>
  action(() => {
    const n = read(issued);
    issued.set(n + 1);
    if (read(current) === null) current.set(n);
    else queue.update((q) => [...q, n]);
  });
// reads only current, so its input does not change while it waits
const loaded = async (fetch: Fetch, current: number | null): Promise<Answer | null> => {
  if (current === null) return null;
  try {
    const r = await fetch(\`https://example.invalid/greeting?n=\${current}\`);
    return { n: current, text: r.ok ? await r.text() : null };
  } catch (e) {
    return { n: current, text: null };
  }
};
const arrived = function* (loaded: Answer | null) {
  if (loaded !== null) yield loaded;
};
const greeting = (): StateCell<string[]> => state<string[]>([]);
// records the answer and starts the next request
const store = (greeting: State<string[]>, current: State<number | null>, queue: State<number[]>) =>
  action((a: Answer) => {
    if (a.n !== read(current)) return;
    greeting.update((g) => [...g, a.text ?? "(failed)"]);
    const q = read(queue);
    const head = q[0];
    if (head !== undefined) {
      current.set(head);
      queue.set(q.slice(1));
    } else current.set(null);
  });
const onArrived = (arrived: Events<Answer>, store: Action<[Answer]>) => on(arrived, store);`
// %%
md`Every answer, in order. Only one request is in flight at a time. An ask made during that time waits in \`queue\`, and \`store\` starts the next request. A failed fetch is recorded as "(failed)", and the queue continues.

\`\`\`ts
${spec_s6_everyAnswer}
\`\`\``
// %%
md`**S6.13** A top-level call is an action call made while no function of an action runs. An event handler, a listener that delivers a value, or JavaScript makes it. A top-level call runs to completion before its caller continues. An action called while a function of an action runs is part of that call. A call of an \`Action\` has the type \`void\` and returns \`undefined\`, whatever its function returns (since 2026-11-24, y1).`
// %%
md`**S6.14** The writes of a top-level call, with the writes of the actions that it calls, commit together one time, after the call returns. Calls that return before that commit share the commit. A reader thus runs one time for them and sees none of the values between. A call that throws keeps none of its writes (S6.10). Inside the call, \`read(r)\` is the committed value with the earlier writes of the call (S6.8).`
// %%
md`S6.15 Removed 2026-11-20: actions are synchronous (S6.12).`
// %%
md`**S6.16** A throw from a top-level call goes to its caller.`
// %%
md`S6.17 Removed 2026-11-20: actions are synchronous (S6.12).`
// %%
md`**S6.18** The content type of a state cell cannot admit \`undefined\`. It cannot be \`undefined\`, \`void\` or \`unknown\`, or a union with one of them, directly or through a type alias. That is \`[state-undefined]\`. A property of the content can be optional. The value variable of a state cell is the Observable \`Generators.input\` of its handle, which skips an \`undefined\` value. The cell would then have no value. Use \`null\`. \`State\`, \`StateCell\` and \`Events\` with no type argument are \`[type]\`.`
// %%
md`**S6.19** A function made in the body of an action can write, read and call \`Action\`s only where it runs before the function of the action returns. It can be:

- called in the body, directly or through a local function that is only called;
- given to an array method that calls it before the method returns, such as \`forEach\` or \`map\`;
- given to \`update\`.

The function can run after the call when it is returned, stored, put in a record, an array or a state cell, given to \`.then\`, to a function of the module or to an intrinsic that keeps it (such as \`Map.set\`), or when it is async or a generator. Its \`set\`, \`update\`, \`read\` and \`Action\` calls are then \`[state-write]\`, \`[read-outside-action]\` and \`[action-call]\`, as in S6.5, S6.8 and S6.6. A \`set\` or \`update\` taken as a value is a function that writes, and the same rule applies to it.`
// %%
md`**S6.20** A \`key\` attribute on the element of a component is for the renderer, as on an intrinsic element. It is a \`string | number\`, and the checker does not check it against the props of the component (since 2026-11-23; before that, it was \`[excess-property]\`). The component does not get \`key\`: \`h\` omits it from the props, as React does (since 2026-11-23, a9). A component whose props type declares \`key\` is \`[type]\`. Give the prop a different name, such as \`id\`.

Only JSX makes an \`Elem\`. A record with the fields of an element is not an \`Elem\`. Such a record is \`[type]\` where an \`Elem\` or a JSX child is expected, and as the return value of a component. The renderer draws only elements that JSX made. It draws any other value as text, also a record of the same structure. A primitive is drawn as \`String\` gives it, and an object as \`[object Object]\`, \`[object Array]\` or \`[object Function]\`. The renderer calls no function in such a value. This includes its own \`toString\`, \`valueOf\` and event props.`
// %%
md`These are the cells of this section. \`s6_copy\` reads \`s6_count\` when it is called. \`s6_bumpThenCopy\` writes \`s6_count\` and calls \`s6_copy\`, which sees that write (S6.8). \`s6_controls\` holds two actions in a record. \`s6_view\` has a button for each of \`s6_increment\` and \`s6_bumpThenCopy\`, and shows both counts.`
// %%
//ss: ./spec-6.tsx
// %%
