// %%
md`## 7. Events and listeners

An events cell is a generator whose values are delivered one at a time. A listener is a cell that connects an events cell to an action.`
// %%
md`**S7.1** An input \`t: Events<T>\` gets a handle to the sync generator cell \`t\` that yields \`T\`. It does not get the values, and it is not a dependency on them. \`Events<T>\` is \`[inject-events]\` on a cell that is not a sync generator that yields \`T\`. This includes an async generator. Since 2026-11-23, the value that the generator yields only has to be assignable to \`T\`, because its values go only to the listener. When a cell takes \`t\` as \`Events\`, an input that reads \`t\` without \`Events\` is \`[inject-events]\`.`
// %%
md`**S7.2** \`on(t, a)\` has the type \`Listener\`. \`a\` must be an \`Action\` that accepts the values that \`t\` yields. If not, it is \`[type]\`.`
// %%
md`**S7.3** A \`Listener\` can only be the full value of a sync cell that is not a generator. It is the value that the cell returns, or a branch of a conditional that the cell returns when every branch is a \`Listener\`. In any other position it is \`[listener-placement]\`: in an array, a record or a state cell, as an argument, in a nested function, in a union with another type, in an async or generator cell, and as the input of another cell.`
// %%
md`**S7.4** While a listener is the value of its cell, its action runs one time for each value that the events cell yields, in order. The listener ends when:

- its cell is deleted or rejected;
- the variables of its module are deleted;
- its cell runs again with other events or another action.

A run with the same events and the same action keeps the listener. A listener cell that runs again, for any reason, has one listener after the run. No value is lost or delivered two times, whatever the order of that run, a commit and the value.`
// %%
md`**S7.5** Dataflow starts and stops events. The events cell takes inputs and yields nothing when it must not run. When an input changes (\`Object.is\`, as in S6.2), the events cell runs again from its start and keeps its handle. When it runs again with the same inputs, it keeps its generator. An events cell writes nothing (S6.5).`
// %%
md`**S7.6** When the action of a listener throws, that call keeps none of its writes (S6.10). The next value is still delivered. The console reports the error with the names of the listener, the action and the events cell.`
// %%
md`**S7.7** Two listener cells on one events cell get each value in the order of the cells in their module. An edit of the first cell does not change that order.`
// %%
md`**S7.8** An \`Events<T>\` and a \`Listener\` have no members. A read of one, by a member, \`?.\`, an index or a destructuring pattern, is \`[type]\`. A \`Listener\` can only be the full value of a cell (S7.3), so a member read on a \`Listener\` is also \`[listener-placement]\`.`
// %%
md`These are the cells of this section. \`s7_onBeat\` runs \`s7_add\` for each of the three values that \`s7_beats\` yields. Thus \`s7_seen\` becomes \`[1, 2, 3]\`.`
// %%
//ss: ./spec-7.ts
// %%
spec_rejects_7 = [
  { statement: "S7.1", rule: "inject-events", source: "const ticks = async function* () {\n  yield 1;\n};\nconst tick = () => action((n: number) => {});\nconst on1 = (ticks: Events<number>, tick: Action<[number]>) => on(ticks, tick);" },
  { statement: "S7.1", rule: "inject-events", source: "const ticks = () => 1;\nconst on1 = (ticks: Events<number>) => 1;" },
  { statement: "S7.1", rule: "inject-events", source: "const ticks = function* () {\n  yield 1;\n};\nconst tick = () => action((n: number) => {});\nconst on1 = (ticks: Events<number>, tick: Action<[number]>) => on(ticks, tick);\nconst reader = (ticks: number) => ticks;" },
  { statement: "S7.2", rule: "type", source: "const ticks = function* () {\n  yield 1;\n};\nconst tick = () => action((s: string) => {});\nconst on1 = (ticks: Events<number>, tick: Action<[string]>) => on(ticks, tick);" },
  { statement: "S7.3", rule: "listener-placement", source: "const ticks = function* () {\n  yield 1;\n};\nconst tick = () => action((n: number) => {});\nconst on1 = (ticks: Events<number>, tick: Action<[number]>) => [on(ticks, tick)];" },
  { statement: "S7.3", rule: "listener-placement", source: "const ticks = function* () {\n  yield 1;\n};\nconst on1 = (ticks: Events<number>) => () => on(ticks, action((n: number) => {}));" },
  { statement: "S7.3", rule: "listener-placement", source: "const ticks = function* () {\n  yield 1;\n};\nconst tick = () => action((n: number) => {});\nconst on1 = (ticks: Events<number>, tick: Action<[number]>) => ({ l: on(ticks, tick) });" },
  { statement: "S7.3", rule: "listener-placement", source: "const ticks = function* () {\n  yield 1;\n};\nconst tick = () => action((n: number) => {});\nconst on1 = (ticks: Events<number>, tick: Action<[number]>) => state(on(ticks, tick));" },
  { statement: "S7.3", rule: "listener-placement", source: "const ticks = function* () {\n  yield 1;\n};\nconst tick = () => action((n: number) => {});\nconst on1 = (ticks: Events<number>, tick: Action<[number]>) => {\n  const g = (x: Listener) => 1;\n  return g(on(ticks, tick));\n};" },
  { statement: "S7.3", rule: "listener-placement", source: "const ticks = function* () {\n  yield 1;\n};\nconst tick = () => action((n: number) => {});\nconst flag = () => true;\nconst on1 = (ticks: Events<number>, tick: Action<[number]>, flag: boolean) => (flag ? on(ticks, tick) : null);" },
  { statement: "S7.3", rule: "listener-placement", source: "const ticks = function* () {\n  yield 1;\n};\nconst tick = () => action((n: number) => {});\nconst on1 = async (ticks: Events<number>, tick: Action<[number]>) => on(ticks, tick);" },
  { statement: "S7.3", rule: "listener-placement", source: "const ticks = function* () {\n  yield 1;\n};\nconst tick = () => action((n: number) => {});\nconst on1 = function* (ticks: Events<number>, tick: Action<[number]>) {\n  yield on(ticks, tick);\n};" },
  { statement: "S7.3", rule: "listener-placement", source: "const ticks = function* () {\n  yield 1;\n};\nconst tick = () => action((n: number) => {});\nconst on1 = (ticks: Events<number>, tick: Action<[number]>) => on(ticks, tick);\nconst x = (on1) => 1;" },
  { statement: "S7.8", rule: "type", source: "const ticks = function* () {\n  yield 1;\n};\nconst n = (ticks: Events<number>) => ticks.events;" },
  { statement: "S7.8", rule: "type", source: "const ticks = function* () {\n  yield 1;\n};\nconst n = (ticks: Events<number>) => {\n  const { events } = ticks;\n  return events;\n};" }
]
// %%
spec_rejects_7_view = spec_rejectsTable(spec_rejects_7)
// %%
spec_stream = (n, { throwEvery = 0 } = {}) => [
  // the cells of a module whose listener records each value of nums: count, last, gaps (a value that is
  // not the last plus one) and sum; toggle stops and starts nums; throwEvery makes every k-th call throw
  "type S = { readonly count: number; readonly last: number; readonly gaps: number; readonly sum: number };",
  "const seen = (): StateCell<S> => state<S>({ count: 0, last: 0, gaps: 0, sum: 0 });",
  "const running = (): StateCell<boolean> => state(true);",
  `const nums = function* (running: boolean) {\n  if (!running) return;\n  for (let i = 1; i <= ${n}; i++) yield i;\n};`,
  "const weight = () => (v: number) => v;",
  throwEvery
    ? `const record = (seen: State<S>, weight: (v: number) => number) =>\n  action((v: number) => {\n    seen.update((s) => ({ count: s.count + 1, last: v, gaps: s.gaps + (v === s.last + 1 ? 0 : 1), sum: s.sum + weight(v) }));\n    if (v % ${throwEvery} === 0) throw new Error(\`no \${v}\`);\n  });`
    : "const record = (seen: State<S>, weight: (v: number) => number) =>\n  action((v: number) => seen.update((s) => ({ count: s.count + 1, last: v, gaps: s.gaps + (v === s.last + 1 ? 0 : 1), sum: s.sum + weight(v) })));",
  "const onNum = (nums: Events<number>, record: Action<[number]>) => on(nums, record);",
  "const toggle = (running: State<boolean>) => action(() => running.update((r) => !r));"
]
// %%
test_spec_S7_1 = {
  spec_is(spec_okCells("const ticks = function* () {\n  yield 1;\n};\nconst tick = () => action((n: number) => {});\nconst on1 = (ticks: Events<number>, tick: Action<[number]>) => on(ticks, tick);\nconst s = (): StateCell<number> => state(0);\nconst t = (s: State<number>, ticks: Events<number>) => action(() => s.set(1));"), "ticks:ok,tick:ok,on1:ok,s:ok,t:ok");
  return `${spec_rejectsFor("S7.1")}; ${spec_acceptsFor("S7.1")}; a handle may also be taken by a cell that is not a listener`;
}
// %%
test_spec_S7_2 = spec_rejectsFor("S7.2")
// %%
test_spec_S7_3 = {
  spec_is(spec_okCells("const ticks = function* () {\n  yield 1;\n};\nconst tick = () => action((n: number) => {});\nconst flag = () => true;\nconst on1 = (ticks: Events<number>, tick: Action<[number]>, flag: boolean) => (flag ? on(ticks, tick) : on(ticks, tick));"), "ticks:ok,tick:ok,flag:ok,on1:ok");
  if (!(await spec_frames(async () => JSON.stringify(await spec_module.value("s7_seen")) === "[1,2,3]"))) throw new Error(`s7_seen is ${JSON.stringify(await spec_module.value("s7_seen"))}`);
  return `${spec_rejectsFor("S7.3")}; a conditional of two listeners is accepted; s7_seen is [1,2,3]`;
}
// %%
test_spec_S7_4 = {
  const A = await spec_editor(spec_stream(60));
  try {
    if (!(await spec_frames(async () => (await A.value("seen")).count >= 60))) throw new Error(`seen ${JSON.stringify(await A.value("seen"))}`);
    for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) await new Promise((r) => requestAnimationFrame(r));
    await spec_flush();
    spec_is(await A.value("seen"), { count: 60, last: 60, gaps: 0, sum: 1830 });
  } finally { A.dispose(); }
  // a long stream: the listener edited, its action's input edited, an input of nums run again with the same
  // value (nums keeps its generator and handle, and onNum runs again on equal inputs), then the listener deleted
  const long = spec_stream(1000000).flatMap((t) => soundscript.declaredName(t) !== "nums" ? [t] : ["const limit = () => 1000000;", "const nums = function* (running: boolean, limit: number) {\n  if (!running) return;\n  for (let i = 1; i <= limit; i++) yield i;\n};"]);
  const B = await spec_editor(long);
  try {
    const count = async () => (await B.value("seen")).count;
    const exact = async () => { const s = await B.value("seen"); if (s.gaps !== 0 || s.count !== s.last) throw new Error(`a value lost or doubled: ${JSON.stringify(s)}`); return s.count; };
    await spec_frames(async () => (await count()) >= 10);
    await B.edit("onNum", "const onNum = (nums: Events<number>, record: Action<[number]>) => /* again */ on(nums, record);");
    const at1 = await count();
    await spec_frames(async () => (await count()) >= at1 + 10);
    await exact();
    await B.edit("weight", "const weight = () => (v: number) => v * 1;");
    const at2 = await count();
    await spec_frames(async () => (await count()) >= at2 + 10);
    await exact();
    const handle = await B.value("nums"), onNum0 = await B.evals("onNum");
    await B.edit("limit", "const limit = () => /* again */ 1000000;");
    const at3 = await count();
    await spec_frames(async () => (await count()) >= at3 + 10);
    const n = await exact();
    const again = (await B.evals("onNum")) - onNum0;
    spec_is([again > 0, (await B.value("nums")) === handle, soundscript.running([handle])], [true, true, 1]);
    B.variable("onNum").delete();
    // the deletion reaches the listener through onCodeChange, at a later runtime compute; until then it is pulled
    if (!(await spec_frames(async () => soundscript.running([handle]) === 0))) throw new Error("onNum deleted, its listener still attached");
    await spec_flush();
    const stopped = await count();
    for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) await new Promise((r) => requestAnimationFrame(r));
    spec_is(await count(), stopped);
    return `60 values, each once in order; after three edits (the last ran onNum again ${again} times on equal inputs) ${n} values with none lost or delivered twice, one listener; deleted, it delivers nothing`;
  } finally { B.dispose(); }
}
// %%
test_spec_S7_5 = {
  const E = await spec_editor(spec_stream(1000000));
  try {
    const seen = () => E.value("seen");
    await spec_frames(async () => (await seen()).count >= 10);
    const listener = await E.evals("onNum"), events = await E.evals("nums"), handle = await E.value("nums");
    const toggle = await E.value("toggle");
    toggle();
    await spec_flush();
    const c1 = (await seen()).count;
    for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) await new Promise((r) => requestAnimationFrame(r));
    spec_is((await seen()).count, c1);
    toggle();
    await spec_flush();
    if (!(await spec_frames(async () => (await seen()).count >= c1 + 10))) throw new Error("did not start again");
    const s = await seen();
    // started again from 1: one gap, where the values went back to 1
    spec_is(s.gaps, 1);
    spec_is([(await E.evals("nums")) - events, (await E.value("nums")) === handle], [2, true]);
    return `stopped at ${c1}; started again from 1; nums ran twice more and kept its handle (onNum ran ${(await E.evals("onNum")) - listener} more times)`;
  } finally { E.dispose(); }
}
// %%
test_spec_S7_6 = {
  const said = [], was = console.error;
  let E;
  try {
    // messages from this test's listener are kept; any other goes on to the console
    console.error = (...a) => {
      const m = a.map((x) => (x instanceof Error ? x.message : String(x))).join(" ");
      if (m.includes("listener onNum") && m.includes("events nums")) said.push(m); else was(...a);
    };
    E = await spec_editor(spec_stream(60, { throwEvery: 10 }));
    await spec_frames(async () => (await E.value("seen")).last >= 59);
    for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) await new Promise((r) => requestAnimationFrame(r));
    await spec_flush();
    const s = await E.value("seen");
    spec_is([s.count, s.last], [54, 59]);
    const named = said.filter((m) => m.includes("listener onNum") && m.includes("action record") && m.includes("events nums"));
    spec_is(named.length, 6);
    return `6 of 60 calls threw and kept nothing; the rest were delivered (count 54, last 59); ${JSON.stringify(named[0].slice(0, 80))}`;
  } finally { console.error = was; E?.dispose(); }
}
// %%
test_spec_S7_8 = spec_rejectsFor("S7.8")
// %%
test_spec_S7_7 = {
  const E = await spec_editor([
    "const trail = (): StateCell<readonly string[]> => state<readonly string[]>([]);",
    "const beats = function* () {\n  let i = 0;\n  while (true) yield i++;\n};",
    "const addFirst = (trail: State<readonly string[]>) => action((v: number) => trail.update((l) => [...l, `first ${v}`]));",
    "const addSecond = (trail: State<readonly string[]>) => action((v: number) => trail.update((l) => [...l, `second ${v}`]));",
    // placed first but named last, so place order and name order disagree
    "const zeta = (beats: Events<number>, addFirst: Action<[number]>) => on(beats, addFirst);",
    "const alpha = (beats: Events<number>, addSecond: Action<[number]>) => on(beats, addSecond);"
  ]);
  try {
    const log = () => E.value("trail");
    // who ran on each beat, from the first beat both got (alpha attaches some beats after zeta)
    const runs = (l) => { const by = new Map(); for (const e of l) { const [who, v] = e.split(" "); by.set(v, [...(by.get(v) ?? []), who]); } const all = [...by.values()].map((w) => w.join(",")); return all.slice(all.indexOf("first,second")); };
    await spec_frames(async () => runs(await log()).length >= 4);
    await E.edit("zeta", "const zeta = (addFirst: Action<[number]>, beats: Events<number>) => on(beats, addFirst);");
    const n0 = runs(await log()).length;
    await spec_frames(async () => runs(await log()).length >= n0 + 10);
    const r = runs(await log());
    const off = r.filter((w) => w !== "first,second");
    if (off.length) throw new Error(`beats not run first then second: ${JSON.stringify(off)}`);
    return `zeta (placed first), then alpha, on each of ${r.length} beats, ${r.length - n0} of them after zeta was edited`;
  } finally { E.dispose(); }
}
// %%
md`## 8. Editing a module with broken cells

This section tells you what the notebook does when an edit leaves cells that the checker rejects. The notebook always stores the edit. A rejected cell does not run.`
// %%
md`**S8.1** Every edit is stored, also an edit that does not parse. There is one exception: an edit whose text names no cell is not stored. Such a text has no \`const <name>\`, \`let <name>\` or function declaration (S1.1) at its start, after comments and an optional \`export\`. An edit that takes the name of another cell is stored with a different name (S1.6).`
// %%
md`**S8.2** After each edit, every cell is ok, rejected or blocked.

- A rejected cell breaks a rule of its own, does not parse (\`[parse]\`), or uses a construct that the checker has no rule for (\`[unsupported]\`, such as the array method \`flat\`). A cell whose check fails because the type of an input changed is rejected with its own message.
- A blocked cell breaks no rule of its own. It takes a cell that is not ok, as a value or as \`State<T>\`.`
// %%
md`**S8.3** Only ok cells run. An edit and every other cell whose compiled form the edit changes are defined in one step. Thus no cell runs between them on a mix of old and new definitions. A rejected cell raises \`<name> does not check: [rule] …\`. Its body never runs, at load or after an edit. The body is kept only as source. A blocked cell fails with the error of the input that is not ok.`
// %%
md`**S8.4** A cell whose compiled text an edit did not change is not defined again. Thus the edit itself does not run cells outside the broken part, and a state cell that stays ok keeps its value. In all other cases the rule of the runtime decides when a cell runs: a cell runs again when one of its inputs was computed again, also when the new value is equal.`
// %%
md`**S8.5** A state cell keeps its value only through the last value of its variable (S6.2). The runtime clears the last value of a variable in error. Thus a state cell that was in error starts again from its initial value. A state cell was in error when an input blocked it, when an input threw, or when it was rejected and later repaired. The runtime has one exception: when a newer run replaced the failed run before the failed run settled, the last value is not cleared. S6.2 then applies as if there was no error. Which of the two occurs is not specified. A state cell that must survive an error should not have inputs that can fail.`
// %%
md`**S8.6** A listener in the broken part ends when the edit is applied. Nothing is delivered to it after that. When the part is repaired, the listener attaches again, one time.`
// %%
md`**S8.7** \`compile\` does not run an island that holds a cell that is not ok. An island of ok cells runs when another cell of the module does not parse.`
// %%
spec_editFirst = [
  "const width = () => 640;",
  "const height = () => 400;",
  "const area = (width: number, height: number) => width * height;",
  "const aspect = (width, height) => (width / height).toFixed(2);",
  "const half = (area: number) => area / 2;",
  "const count = (): StateCell<number> => state(0);",
  "const increment = (count: State<number>) => action(() => count.update((n) => n + 1));",
  "const label = (count: number) => `pressed ${count}`;",
  "const tall = (height: number) => height * 2;"
]
// %%
test_spec_S8_1 = {
  const E = await spec_editor(["const a = () => 1;"]);
  try {
    for (const text of ["1 + 1;", "a();", "// a comment"]) {
      const r = ss_language.check(text, [], E.at);
      if (r.store) throw new Error(`${JSON.stringify(text)} would be stored`);
    }
    for (const text of ["const b = () =>", "export const c = () => 2;", "/* note */ function d() { return 3; }"]) {
      const r = ss_language.check(text, [], E.at);
      if (!r.store) throw new Error(`${JSON.stringify(text)} would not be stored: ${JSON.stringify(r.problems)}`);
    }
    return "an edit naming no cell is not stored; an unparsable one, an exported one and one after a comment are";
  } finally { E.dispose(); }
}
// %%
test_spec_S8_2 = {
  const E = await spec_editor(spec_editFirst);
  try {
    await E.edit("width", 'const width = () => "800";');
    spec_is(E.states(), { width: "ok", height: "ok", area: "rejected", aspect: "rejected", half: "blocked by area", count: "ok", increment: "ok", label: "ok", tall: "ok" });
    await E.edit("tall", "const tall = (height: number) => height *;");
    spec_is(E.states().tall, "rejected");
    await ss_language.apply("const w = () => [[1], [2]].flat();", [], E.at);
    spec_is(E.states().w, "rejected");
    const w = (await E.value("w")).error ?? "";
    if (!/^w does not check: \[unsupported\]/.test(w)) throw new Error(`w: ${JSON.stringify(w)}`);
    return `area and aspect are rejected by their own checks when width became a string; half is blocked by area; tall does not parse and is rejected; w uses flat and is rejected: ${w}`;
  } finally { E.dispose(); }
}
// %%
test_spec_S8_3 = {
  globalThis.__specSneak = 0;
  const E = await spec_editor([...spec_editFirst, "const sneak = (width: number) => { (globalThis as any).__specSneak++; return width; };"]);
  try {
    const sneak = await E.value("sneak");
    if (!/^sneak does not check: \[/.test(sneak.error ?? "") || globalThis.__specSneak !== 0) throw new Error(`at load: ${JSON.stringify(sneak)}, ran ${globalThis.__specSneak}`);
    const edited = "const sneak = (width: number, height: number) => { (globalThis as any).__specSneak++; return width + height; };";
    await E.edit("sneak", edited);
    if (!/^sneak does not check: \[/.test((await E.value("sneak")).error ?? "") || globalThis.__specSneak !== 0) throw new Error(`after an edit: ran ${globalThis.__specSneak}`);
    spec_is(E.text("sneak"), edited);
    await E.edit("width", 'const width = () => "800";');
    const area = (await E.value("area")).error ?? "", half = (await E.value("half")).error ?? "";
    if (!/^area does not check: \[inject-type\]/.test(area) || !/area does not check/.test(half)) throw new Error(`area ${area}, half ${half}`);
    await E.edit("tall", "const tall = (height: number) => height *;");
    if (!/^tall does not check: \[parse\]/.test((await E.value("tall")).error ?? "")) throw new Error("tall");
    spec_is(await E.value("label"), "pressed 0");
    await E.edit("tall", "const tall = (height: number) => height * 3;");
    spec_is(await E.value("tall"), 1200);
    return "a rejected body never ran and is kept as source; area raises its message and half fails with it; an unparsable edit raises [parse] while the rest run, and an edit repairs it";
  } finally { E.dispose(); delete globalThis.__specSneak; }
}
// %%
test_spec_S8_4 = {
  const E = await spec_editor(spec_editFirst);
  try {
    (await E.value("increment"))();
    if (!(await spec_frames(async () => (await E.value("label")) === "pressed 1"))) throw new Error("the write did not reach label");
    const runs = () => Promise.all(["height", "tall", "label", "count"].map((n) => E.evals(n)));
    const before = await runs();
    await E.edit("width", 'const width = () => "800";');
    await E.edit("area", "const area = (width: string, height: number) => Number(width) * height;");
    await E.edit("aspect", "const aspect = (width: string, height: number) => (Number(width) / height).toFixed(2);");
    spec_is(Object.values(E.states()).every((x) => x === "ok"), true);
    spec_is([await E.value("area"), await E.value("half"), await E.value("aspect"), await E.value("count")], [320000, 160000, "2.00", 1]);
    spec_is(await runs(), before);
    return "broken and repaired in three edits; height, tall, label and count did not run again, and count kept 1";
  } finally { E.dispose(); }
}
// %%
test_spec_S8_5 = {
  const E = await spec_editor([
    "const base = () => 5;",
    "const seeded = (base: number): StateCell<number> => state(base);",
    "const own = (): StateCell<number> => state(0);",
    "const bump = (seeded: State<number>, own: State<number>) => action(() => { seeded.update((n) => n + 1); own.update((n) => n + 1); });",
    "const sq = (base: number) => base * base;",
    "const fromSq = (sq: number): StateCell<number> => state(sq);",
    "const bumpSq = (fromSq: State<number>) => action(() => fromSq.update((n) => n + 1));"
  ]);
  try {
    (await E.value("bumpSq"))();
    (await E.value("bump"))(); (await E.value("bump"))();
    await spec_flush();
    spec_is([await E.value("seeded"), await E.value("own"), await E.value("fromSq")], [7, 2, 26]);
    await E.edit("base", 'const base = () => "5";');
    await E.edit("own", 'const own = (): StateCell<number> => state("0");');
    spec_is([E.states().fromSq, E.states().own], ["blocked by sq", "rejected"]);
    await E.edit("base", "const base = () => 5;");
    await E.edit("own", "const own = (): StateCell<number> => state(0);");
    // each started again, or (the failed run replaced before it settled) kept what was written
    const after = [await E.value("fromSq"), await E.value("own"), await E.value("seeded")];
    const either = [[25, 26], [0, 2], [5, 7]];
    if (!after.every((v, i) => either[i].includes(v))) throw new Error(`after the repair: ${JSON.stringify(after)}`);
    await E.edit("base", "const base = () => 6;");
    spec_is(await E.value("fromSq"), 36);
    return `after the repair fromSq, own and seeded are ${JSON.stringify(after)} (each its initial value or the value written); fromSq starts again at 36 from 6`;
  } finally { E.dispose(); }
}
// %%
test_spec_S8_6 = {
  const E = await spec_editor([
    "const seen = (): StateCell<{ readonly count: number; readonly last: number; readonly sum: number }> => state({ count: 0, last: 0, sum: 0 });",
    "const by = () => 1;",
    "const nums = function* () {\n  for (let i = 1; i < 1000000; i++) yield i;\n};",
    "const add = (seen: State<{ readonly count: number; readonly last: number; readonly sum: number }>, by: number) => action((v: number) => seen.update((s) => ({ count: s.count + 1, last: v, sum: s.sum + by })));",
    "const onNum = (nums: Events<number>, add: Action<[number]>) => on(nums, add);"
  ]);
  try {
    const seen = () => E.value("seen");
    await spec_frames(async () => (await seen()).count >= 5);
    const before = await seen();
    await E.edit("by", 'const by = () => "1";');
    spec_is([E.states().add, E.states().onNum], ["rejected", "blocked by add"]);
    await spec_flush();
    const broken = await seen();
    // a string sum would mean add ran with by already "1": the edit and add's rejected definition are one step (S8.3)
    if (typeof broken.sum !== "number") throw new Error(`add ran on the edited input: ${JSON.stringify(broken)}`);
    const during = `${broken.count - before.count} call(s) from the start of the edit to its end, sum ${broken.sum}`;
    for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) await new Promise((r) => requestAnimationFrame(r));
    spec_is(await seen(), broken);
    await E.edit("by", "const by = () => 2;");
    await spec_frames(async () => (await seen()).count >= broken.count + 10);
    const s = await seen();
    // once each: as many calls as values since the repair, each adding by = 2
    if (s.count - broken.count > s.last - broken.last) throw new Error(`more calls than values since the repair: ${JSON.stringify([broken, s])}`);
    return `nothing delivered while add was rejected (count held at ${broken.count}); after the repair one listener, ${s.count - broken.count} calls for ${s.last - broken.last} values; ${during}`;
  } finally { E.dispose(); }
}
// %%
test_spec_S8_7 = {
  const texts = ["const x = () => 1;", "const y = (x: number) => x + 1;", "const bad = (x: number) => x +;", "const z = (bad: number) => bad;", "const go = (compile: Compile) => compile({ inputs: [\"x\"], refs: [], outputs: [\"y\"] });"];
  const c = soundscript.compiler(() => soundscript.joinCells(texts));
  const r = await c.for("go", {})({ inputs: ["x"], refs: [], outputs: ["y"] })({ x: 41 });
  spec_is(r.y, 42);
  let refused = "";
  try { c.for("go", {})({ inputs: ["bad"], refs: [], outputs: ["z"] }); } catch (e) { refused = e.message; }
  if (!/not ok by the checker/.test(refused)) throw new Error(`gave ${JSON.stringify(refused)}`);
  return `the island x, y runs (y is 42) beside an unparsable cell; ${refused}`;
}
// %%
md`## 9. Foreign values and the shell

This section is about values from code that the checker does not check. It is for a tool that runs the checker outside a notebook. A notebook module has no shell cells. Section 11 gives its boundary to other code.`
// %%
md`**S9.1** A shell cell is a cell that is not checked, which the checker gets together with the checked cells. A notebook module has none (S1.7). To a checked cell, the value of a shell cell is \`unknown\`. An input with no annotation, or with the annotation \`unknown\` or its alias \`Js\`, gets \`unknown\`. \`any\` is \`[no-any]\`. Any other annotation is \`[inject-type]\`, except a function type: the function crosses as a function from another language does (S11.3 to S11.11).`
// %%
md`**S9.2** A checked function from \`unknown\` that narrows with \`typeof\`, \`Array.isArray\` and \`in\` and makes a new value is accepted. It changes a value from a shell cell, or from another language (section 11), into a typed value. On a union, \`"k" in v\` removes no member. A value of a member that does not declare \`k\` can still have \`k\`. That member stays, with \`k\` of type \`unknown\`.`
// %%
md`**S9.3** A foreign declaration (\`declare const\` or \`declare function\`, given to the checker together with the cells) is the trusted signature of an imported cell or library. Each has the mark \`/** pure */\` or \`/** effect */\`. A declaration with no mark is \`[foreign-unmarked]\`. A checked cell that takes an \`effect\` declaration is \`[foreign-effect]\`. The name of a foreign declaration is used before a global of the same name. A notebook module has no foreign declarations.`
// %%
md`**S9.4** The narrowings of one value combine in any order. A test that excludes \`null\` or \`undefined\` (\`v !== null\`, \`v != null\`, \`!!v\`) and a later \`typeof v === "object"\` narrow \`v\` to \`object\`, as when \`typeof\` is tested first. An assignment to \`v\` ends the exclusion.`
// %%
md`**S9.5** The right operand of \`in\` must be an object. An operand of type \`unknown\`, of a type parameter, or of a type that can be a primitive is \`[type]\`. An operand that can be \`null\` or \`undefined\` is \`[possibly-undefined]\`. \`"k" in v\` throws at run time when \`v\` is a primitive. Test \`typeof v === "object" && v !== null\` first.`
// %%
md`**S9.6** \`typeof v === "function"\` narrows a \`v\` of type \`unknown\`, or of a type parameter, to \`object\`. The test shows that \`v\` is not a primitive. It does not show which arguments \`v\` takes. \`object\` has no members and no call signature. Thus a call of it, or its use where a function or an \`Action\` is expected, is \`[type]\`. \`typeof v === "symbol"\` narrows \`v\` to \`symbol\`. A type with a call signature (a function type, or an interface or alias with one) has the tag \`"function"\`.

An \`Action\` does not get to \`unknown\` (S6.6). A \`State\` or \`Events\` handle given the type \`unknown\`, \`object\` or a type parameter of a function of the module is \`[handle-pass]\`. Since 2026-11-24 (w1), a function, a \`FetchResponse\` or any other value that is not a record is \`[forgets]\` when it gets a record type (S2.9). This also applies to a member of a union, an array element and a type argument.`
// %%
md`**S9.7** \`await v\` is \`unknown\` when \`v\` can be an \`object\`, or a record or interface other than \`Promise\` that declares a \`then\` property. \`await\` calls that \`then\` and gives the value that \`then\` passes on.`
// %%
md`**S9.8** \`cast<T>(v)\` checks a value of any type against \`T\` when it runs. The check is the check that a value from another language gets (S11.4, S11.5). The result is a \`Cast<T>\`:

- \`{ ok: true, value }\`, where \`value\` is a deep-frozen copy of \`v\` that keeps only the properties that \`T\` declares; or
- \`{ ok: false, path, error }\`, where \`path\` names the first part of \`v\` that is not a \`T\`, such as \`value.rows[1].price\`.

\`cast\` reads each property of \`v\` one time. Thus a getter or a proxy cannot give one value to the check and a different value to the copy. A throw during the read of \`v\` gives \`ok: false\`. \`cast\` never gives out \`v\` itself.

\`T\` is the one type argument of the call. A call without it is \`[cast-type]\`. \`T\` can be a type that S11.3 accepts as an annotation, except a function type: \`number\`, \`string\`, \`boolean\`, \`null\`, \`undefined\`, literal types, \`unknown\`, arrays, tuples, object types with no methods and no index signature, unions, and aliases of these (recursive aliases as S11.3 allows). Any other \`T\` is \`[cast-type]\`: a function or a method, \`Mut\`, \`Promise\`, \`Map\`, \`Set\` or another class, an index signature, a type parameter, \`object\`, \`never\`, \`bigint\`.

Since 2026-11-23 (a9), these are also \`[cast-type]\`:

- a literal type that is not a finite number, such as \`1e400\` (\`Infinity\`), because JSON writes it as \`null\` in the compiled cell;
- a \`T\` whose run-time form has more than 10000 parts. An alias that is not recursive is written in full at each use. For example, \`type P<X> = { a: X; b: X }\` nested 20 deep has about 2^20 parts.

A body can only call \`cast\` (\`[prelude-value]\`). The copy is not \`Mut\`, so a write to it is \`[member-write]\` (S4.3). The compiler adds the run-time form of \`T\` to each call, from the check of the cell. A cell compiled without its check gives \`ok: false\` for every value.`
// %%
md`These are the cells of this section. \`s9_raw\` is Observable JavaScript. \`s9_size\` takes it as \`unknown\`, and \`s9_decodeSize\` makes a typed \`{ w, h }\` from it.`
