// %%
md`## 10. Tests

A test is a cell.`
// %%
md`**S10.1** A cell whose name starts with \`test_\` is a test. The checker and the compiler treat it as any other cell.`
// %%
md`**S10.2** A test passes when its value resolves. It fails when it throws or rejects. A test that has not settled has not passed.`
// %%
md`These are the cells of this section. \`test_s10_double\` is a test of \`s10_double\`, which it takes as a parameter.`
// %%
//ss: ./spec-10.ts
// %%
spec_rejects_10 = [
  { statement: "S10.1", rule: "free-name", source: "const test_log = () => console;" }
]
// %%
spec_rejects_10_view = spec_rejectsTable(spec_rejects_10)
// %%
test_spec_S10_1 = {
  spec_is(spec_okCells("const a = () => 1;\nconst test_a = (a: number) => {\n  if (a !== 1) throw new Error(\"a\");\n  return \"ok\";\n};"), "a:ok,test_a:ok");
  return `${spec_rejectsFor("S10.1")}; a test takes what it tests as a parameter`;
}
// %%
test_spec_S10_2 = {
  const L = await spec_load("const test_ok = () => \"fine\";\nconst test_fails = () => {\n  throw new Error(\"broken\");\n};\nconst test_later = async () => {\n  await Promise.resolve();\n  throw new Error(\"later\");\n};", {});
  try {
    spec_is(await L.value("test_ok"), "fine");
    const err = async (n) => { try { await L.value(n); return null; } catch (e) { return e.message; } };
    spec_is([await err("test_fails"), await err("test_later")], ["broken", "later"]);
    spec_is(await spec_module.value("test_s10_double"), "s10_double(21) is 42");
    return "test_ok resolves; test_fails throws and test_later rejects; test_s10_double passes";
  } finally { L.dispose(); }
}
// %%
md`## 11. Observable JavaScript and raw cells

A SoundScript cell reads a cell in another language of the same module through a typed parameter. The notebook checks and copies the value when it arrives.`
// %%
md`**S11.1** A parameter can name another variable of the module: an Observable JavaScript cell, a raw cell or an import. If the parameter names no SoundScript cell, foreign declaration, shell cell or capability, it takes the value of that variable. A parameter that names none of these is \`[inject-unknown]\`.`
// %%
md`**S11.2** Such a parameter must have a type annotation. Without one it is \`[boundary-unannotated]\`.`
// %%
md`**S11.3** The annotation can use \`number\`, \`string\`, \`boolean\`, \`null\`, \`undefined\`, literal types, \`unknown\`, arrays, tuples, object types and interfaces with no methods and no index signature, unions, and aliases of these.

A function type, or an alias of one, can be the full annotation when:

- it has no type parameters and no rest parameter;
- its parameters, and its result or the \`T\` of a \`Promise<T>\` result, are annotations that this statement accepts, or \`void\`, which is read as \`undefined\`.

A parameter of the function type can be optional.

Any other annotation is \`[boundary-type]\`: a function type inside an object, array or union, in \`State\`, or as a parameter or result of a function; \`Promise\`, \`Map\`, \`Set\` or another class; \`Events<T>\`.

An alias can refer to itself, directly or through other aliases, when an object type, an array or a tuple is on every path from the alias back to itself, as in \`type Tree = { v: number; kids: Tree[] }\`. \`type A = A | number\` is \`[type-cycle]\` (S1.10, since 2026-11-23). A generic alias whose type arguments are different at every level, such as \`type N<T> = { next: N<T[]> | null }\`, is \`[boundary-type]\`. Since 2026-11-23 (a9), a literal type that is not a finite number (\`1e400\`) and an annotation whose run-time form has more than 10000 parts are \`[boundary-type]\`, as in S9.8.`
// %%
md`**S11.4** The notebook checks each value that arrives against the annotation.

- If the value does not agree with the annotation, the cell that reads it throws a \`TypeError\` that names the place, such as \`rows[1].price: expected number, found string "12"\`.
- A value that reaches itself again at a place that the annotation checks does not agree, for example \`tree.kids[0]: a cycle\`. The check has no depth limit.
- Cells that do not depend on the reading cell are not affected. The reading cell runs again on the next value.
- A function is checked at each call. The call throws a new \`TypeError\` that names the function when the result does not agree, when the function throws, or when the read of its result throws: \`measure(...).n: expected number, found string "3"\` or \`measure(...) threw: boom\`.
- With a \`Promise<T>\` result, the promise that the cell gets rejects with that \`TypeError\` when the result does not agree, when the promise rejects, or when the read of the result throws. A throw from the call itself is thrown at the call.`
// %%
md`**S11.5** The cell gets a deep-frozen copy.

- An object keeps only the properties that its type declares.
- With a recursive alias, an object that the value reaches by two paths, and that both paths read as the same type, is copied one time. Both paths reach the copy.
- \`unknown\` accepts primitives, arrays and plain objects, and copies them in full. A function, a promise or an instance of a class does not agree (S11.4).
- A hole in an array is read as \`undefined\`. It does not agree where the element type has no \`undefined\`. If the type has \`undefined\`, the copy has \`undefined\` at that position.
- A function is given as a frozen function that the boundary makes. At each call, this function gives the function of the other language a deep-frozen copy of each argument. Thus the other language never holds a value that the cell holds. The boundary function calls the function of the other language with no \`this\`, and gives the cell a deep-frozen copy of the result.`
// %%
md`**S11.6** The notebook makes a copy for each value that arrives. Thus the cell sees a value that the other language changed in place and delivered again. The reading cell runs for each value that arrives, as the runtime runs any cell whose input was computed again. An equal copy does not stop the run. The same function delivered again with the same annotation is the same function to the cell. A function delivered after a different function is a new function to the cell, even if it was delivered before.`
// %%
md`**S11.7** \`x: State<T>\` takes the view of an Observable JavaScript \`viewof x\`, or the handle of a SoundScript state cell imported from another module with \`import {viewof x} from …\`. Each write is checked against \`T\`. \`State\` is \`[boundary-state]\` on any other cell in another language. It is also \`[boundary-state]\` when two parameters in one module take the same view or state cell as \`State\` of different types.`
// %%
md`**S11.8** The view holds the value. A write through the \`State\` commits with its action: it sets the \`value\` of the view and dispatches an \`input\` event. \`read\` inside an action gives the value that the control holds at that time, with the earlier writes of the call. The control can round or clamp the value. A change that the user makes on the control is not an action call.`
// %%
md`**S11.9** The value of a cell in another language is state. An action cannot use it (\`[action-capture]\`, section 6). Take the view as \`State\` and \`read\` it inside the action. When the cell has no view, give the value to the action as an argument.

A function from another language is such a value. An action cannot capture it, and cannot capture a cell that reads it. A cell that takes the function, or a cell that reads it, cannot hold a value whose type can be or hold a function in a state cell, and cannot yield one from an events cell (\`[action-capture]\`). Since 2026-11-24 (w1), such a function given a record type (\`{}\`, a record of optional members, an empty interface) is \`[forgets]\` (S2.9), before the capture rule applies.`
// %%
md`**S11.10** When a cell in another language is added, deleted or renamed, the checker checks the SoundScript cells of the module again.`
// %%
md`**S11.11** A function from another language is trusted. The notebook does not check that the function:

- gives equal results for equal arguments;
- ends;
- writes nothing, by a call of an action or in any other way;
- gives values that mean what the cell uses them for.

The notebook checks S11.4 to S11.9: each result has the declared type and is a frozen copy, the function gets copies, no thrown object gets to the cell as the same object, and no action captures the function. A checked cell that calls such a function is only as pure as the function. Two calls with equal arguments can give different results. An action that the function calls can run the calling cell again.`
// %%
md`These are the cells of this section. \`s11_rows\`, \`viewof s11_level\` and \`s11_measure\` are Observable JavaScript. \`s11_raw_area\` is raw. The \`s11_\` cells after them are SoundScript. \`s11_measured\` calls the function \`s11_measure\`.`
// %%
md`\`s11_late_twice\` takes \`s11_late\`, which no cell of this module defines. Thus it shows an error. It is rejected until \`test_spec_S11_10\` defines \`s11_late\` for a short time (S11.10).`
// %%
s11_rows = [{ price: 1, name: "tea" }, { price: 2.5, name: "cake" }]
// %%
viewof s11_level = Inputs.range([0, 10], { step: 1, value: 5, label: "s11_level" })
// %%
s11_measure = (s) => ({ n: s.length, s })
// %%
define("s11_raw_area", [], function () {
  return 12;
})
